PVD("china_vision_2013-2030",{"docs":["","china2030"],"s":{"1":[[1,"198","4","Domestic Market Expansion and Household Consumption Promotion","China's macroeconomic policies need to encourage the growth of the domestic market rather than continue to focus industrial attention mainly on exports.",""],[1,"225","34","Market Development Measures","Develop effective market;",""]],"2":[[1,"16","5","State and Private Sector Competition Reform","First, rethinking the role of the state and the private sector to encourage increased com- petition in the economy.",""],[1,"23","6","Structural Market Economy Reform Programme","First, implement structural reforms to strengthen the foundations for a market- based economy by redefining the role of gov-ernment; reforming and restructuring state enterprises and banks; developing the private sector; promoting competition; and deepen-ing reforms in the land, labor, and financial markets.",""],[1,"24","2","State Enterprise Reform and Ownership Diversification","the focus will need to be further reforms of state enterprises (including measures to recalibrate the role of public resources, introduce modern corporate governance practices, such as separating ownership from management, and implement gradual ownership diversification where necessary)",""],[1,"24","2","Private Sector Development and Market Entry Reform","private sector development and fewer barriers to entry and exit, and increased competition in all sectors, including in strategic and pillar industries",""],[1,"34","3","Economic Reform and Growth Model Continuation","China's reforms, still ongoing, have facilitated regional concentration of activities and captured agglomeration economies in coastal provinces, encouraged mobility of factors and goods across provinces and with the rest of the world, and established a high savings- and investment-led growth process disciplined by the competitive pressures of globalization.",""],[1,"39","14","Reform Policies for New Growth Drivers","Successful implementation of the reform policies, contained in this report, aimed at finding new growth drivers-increased efficiency in input use, higher human capital investments, increased innovation, and a shift to high-value services-will help China avoid the middle-income trap and maintain an expected average growth rate of between 6 and 7 percent a year in the coming two decades",""],[1,"46","3","Government Role Reorientation and Regulatory Framework Reform","the government needs to withdraw from direct involvement in production, dis- tribution, and resource allocation, it will need to focus greater attention on design- ing and implementing the policy and regu- latory framework that empowers others to participate in economic decision making so that the desired outcome of rapid, inclusive, and sustainable growth is achieved.",""],[1,"46","4","State-Owned Sector Reform","the government will need to accelerate reforms in the state-owned sector",""],[1,"46","4","Private Sector Development","and combine it with further development of the private sector",""],[1,"46","4","Factor Market Reform","It will also need to advance reforms in factor markets (capital, land, and labor)",""],[1,"53","3","Structural Reform and Market System Development","it is imperative, therefore, that China resolve these issues, accelerate structural reforms, and develop a market-based system with sound foundations in which public resources finance the delivery of key public goods and services-while a vigorous private sec- tor plays the more important role of driving growth.",""],[1,"55","5","Domestic Competition and Private Sector Reform Package","To increase competition in domestic markets, further reforms will be needed to support private sector firms, such as lowering barriers to firm entry and exit, breaking up state monopolies or oligopolies in key industries (petroleum, chemicals, electricity distribution, and telecommunications), promoting the growth of dynamic SMEs and increasing their access to finance, stimulating much needed regional and local specialization, and encouraging spontaneous state enterprise reforms through competition.",""],[1,"56","1","Independent Oversight of Natural Monopolies","the monopolist enterprise should be subject to independent and strict oversight to ensure that the lack of competition does not lead to monopoly pricing and the abuse of market power that could harm downstream industries.",""],[1,"56","1","Leveling the Competitive Playing Field","Further efforts are also needed to level the playing field, especially between smaller and larger firms, and between state and nonstate firms-with a special focus on laws and regulations, and access to credit from state-owned banks.",""],[1,"56","1","Industrial Policy Modernization for Innovation","This will also require review and modernization of China's \"industrial policies\" to support the growth of firms in dynamic new sectors, improve resource allocation, and stimulate innovation.",""],[1,"56","2","International Competition and Trade Barrier Reduction Measures","competitiveness and innovation will be encouraged by measures to enhance international competition through further integration with the global economy (see chapter 8), and a reduction in behind-the-border barriers to trade.",""],[1,"56","3","Factor Market Reform Package","China needs to also focus attention on factor markets-capital, land, and labor-where market reforms have tended to lag, and con-sequently, the remaining distortions are par-ticularly large.",""],[1,"200","7","State-Owned Enterprise Reform","An emphasis on further reforming SOEs, improving the quality of the workforce, encouraging applied research in firms, strengthening the research infrastructure, and building market institutions to sustain the tempo of competition and facilitate the entry of SMEs may be appropriate.",""],[1,"200","7","Market Institution Building for Competition and SME Entry","An emphasis on further reforming SOEs, improving the quality of the workforce, encouraging applied research in firms, strengthening the research infrastructure, and building market institutions to sustain the tempo of competition and facilitate the entry of SMEs may be appropriate.",""],[1,"279","4","State-Owned Enterprise Reform to Reduce Monopolies","Reform state enterprises to eliminate monopolies that limit new entrants to bet- ter promote innovation without excess state market presence.",""],[1,"285","4","State-Owned Enterprise Reform","Medium-term gains will accrue from innovation and changed behaviors, which will come in response to government reforms to SOEs;",""]],"4":[[1,"200","7","Research and Innovation Support for Firms","An emphasis on further reforming SOEs, improving the quality of the workforce, encouraging applied research in firms, strengthening the research infrastructure, and building market institutions to sustain the tempo of competition and facilitate the entry of SMEs may be appropriate.",""],[1,"202","2","Industrial Extension and SME Laboratory Access Support","Strengthening the industrial exten- sion system and providing smaller firms with easier access to laboratory, metrology, testing, and certification facilities would facilitate meeting these standards by smaller firms.",""],[1,"202","4","Research and Development Platform Support for Enterprises","Government can support enterprises in developing technological capabilities and producing innovative products by establishing research and development platforms for the use of those companies.",""],[1,"202","4","Public-Private Partnership Reform for R&D Platforms","It is important to make them more functional and more responsive to the needs of the economy through a public-private partnership approach.",""],[1,"202","4","Replication of Public R&D Platforms","there are some good examples of public platforms that could be more widely replicated.",""]],"5":[[1,"414","2","Adjustment of Industrial FDI Guidelines","First, the Industrial Guidelines for FDI should be adjusted to encourage investments in emerging industries and research and development (R&D).",""],[1,"414","2","Investment Climate Improvement and Equal Treatment Reform","Second, policy should be shifted from giving preferential incentives to improving the investment climate: ensuring a level playing field between domestic and foreign investors, improving the quality and efficiency of public services, improving human resources, and strengthening the service sectors.",""],[1,"414","2","FDI R&D Integration into Domestic Innovation System","Third, to make full use of FDI's spillover effects, FDI's R&D should be integrated into the domestic innovation system.",""],[1,"414","2","Foreign-Domestic Cooperation in Human Resources and R&D","The government should encourage greater cooperation and exchange between foreign companies and domestic institutes and companies related to the human resources, information, and R&D sectors.",""],[1,"414","2","Liberalization of FDI in Services","Finally, the government needs to enhance international competitiveness of its service sectors by further liberalizing FDI in services.",""],[1,"416","6","Private Firm Outward Investment Facilitation","the government should ensure that private firms are given the same opportunity to invest abroad and should avoid burdensome approval pro- cesses that might inhibit such investment.",""],[1,"418","7","Reduction of Foreign Direct Investment Restrictions","Providing pre-entry national treatment would also imply reducing restrictions on FDI inflows.",""],[1,"419","2","FDI Approval and Restriction Rationalization","Rationalizing the system of approvals and restrictions for inward FDI could reduce the costs confronting foreign investors, by pro- viding greater assurance that they would be able to invest without excessive delays and to operate without undue interference.",""],[1,"419","2","Service Sector FDI Liberalization","In par- ticular, reducing restrictions on service sector FDI would promote service sector efficiency,",""],[1,"428","7","FDI Approval Process Deregulation","A further early measure would be to abolish approval processes for inward and outward FDI flows, which tend to be more stable and long term than portfolio flows.",""]],"8":[[1,"191","1","Technological Upgrading and Indigenous Innovation Support","China is redoubling its own efforts at technological upgrading, indigenous innovation,32 and takeover of foreign firms and their brands by China's leading challeng- ers, while supporting determined efforts by Chinese firms to innovate, build their own brand image, and expand their share of global markets.",""],[1,"191","2","National Science and Technology Innovation Strategy","In early 2006, the government announced its National Program Outline for Medium and Long Term Development of Science and Technology (2006-2020).",""],[1,"191","3","National Science and Technology Subprograms","the national program also has 16 specific subprograms-from core electronic components to moon exploration-aimed at achieving advances in core common-use technologies and enhancing the capabilities of Chinese enterprises.",""],[1,"191","5","Strategic Industries Development Initiative","Developing strategic industries such as energy-saving and environmental protection, next-generation information technology, biotechnology, high-end manufacturing, new energy, new materials, and clean energy vehicles.",""],[1,"211","1","R&D Intensification and Green Growth Innovation Programme","The proposed intensification of R&D activities during 2011-20 and the increasing emphasis on achieving technological parity with the West and on greening growth to improve quality and minimize environmental costs offers an opportunity to develop the innovation capacity of coastal and some inland cities and, in the process, to increase the productivity of R&D expenditures.",""],[1,"225","6","Management Improvement and Research Capacity Strengthening","Improve management; Purchase of technology; Long-term R&D investment; Research networking; Recruit talents",""],[1,"225","7","R&D Tax Incentives and Demand-Side Support","Tax incentives for R&D investment; Demands-side incentives;",""],[1,"225","11","Technology Transfer and Local R&D Development","Purchase technology from the parent company; Launch local R&D activities; Hire local talents",""],[1,"225","19","Internal Incentive Mechanism Reform","Design effective internal incentive mechanisms; Recruit first grade scientists and engineers",""],[1,"225","20","Major R&D Project Grant Award Reform","Reform grant award system for major R&D projects; Increase funds for hiring experts and postdoctoral fellows",""],[1,"225","24","Major R&D Project Grant Award Reform","Reform grant award system for major R&D projects; Encourage freedom in research; Reform the appraisal and compensation system",""],[1,"225","24","Research Freedom Promotion","Reform grant award system for major R&D projects; Encourage freedom in research; Reform the appraisal and compensation system",""],[1,"225","24","Research Appraisal and Compensation Reform","Reform grant award system for major R&D projects; Encourage freedom in research; Reform the appraisal and compensation system",""],[1,"225","34","National Innovation System Improvement","Improve national innovation system;",""],[1,"225","34","Firm-Level R&D Promotion","Promote R&D by firms;",""],[1,"225","34","Major R&D Projects","Organize major R&D projects;",""],[1,"225","38","Business Start-up and Innovation Enabling Environment Reform","Improve infrastructure and institutions to create an enabling environment for business start-ups and innovation; Promote R&D by firms; Promote the development of local industrial clusters",""],[1,"225","38","Firm-Level Research and Development Promotion","Promote R&D by firms; Promote the development of local industrial clusters",""],[1,"225","38","Local Industrial Cluster Development Initiative","Promote the development of local industrial clusters",""]],"11":[[1,"185","2","National Research Network and Firm Consortium Development","The central government can help build countrywide research networks to mobilize national talent and create consortia composed of firms from inland and coastal areas to raise the technological levels of all participants through cross-fertilization.",""],[1,"185","2","Integration of Domestic Research Networks into Global Research Networks","The domestic research networks should be incorporated into global research networks so that Chinese companies can also participate in research conducted in other parts of the world.",""],[1,"185","2","Capacity-Building and Anti-Protectionism Measures for Global Research Participation","Such participation, and with it the creation of global research networks, will be promoted by measures that improve internal organizational and technological capacities and by policies that minimize protectionist tendencies in other countries.",""],[1,"185","4","Open Innovation Partnerships with Multinational Corporations","Closer collaboration and partnerships with multinational corporations on the basis of mutual trust and recognition will contribute to the making of a dynamic and open innovation system.",""],[1,"203","16","Countrywide Research Consortium Network","The central government can take greater initiative in building countrywide research networks that mobilize national talent and reduce the relative isolation of inland cities by including firms from the inland cities in research consortia tasked with disseminating the latest technologies and advancing technology in areas where they have an existing or nascent comparative advantage.",""],[1,"211","5","International Innovation Networking and Cluster Development","innovative cities need to actively network with other centers throughout the region and the world and build areas of expertise.",""],[1,"211","5","University and Firm Collaboration for Global Commercial and Scientific Linkages","This calls for embracing a culture of openness, and activism on the part of major local firms and universities to translate such a culture into commercial and scientific linkages that span the globe.",""],[1,"225","26","Government-Industry Communication and R&D Alliance Facilitation","Improve communications between governments and industry; Facilitate R&D alliances",""]],"12":[[1,"184","5","Government Support for Basic Research Funding","An adequate volume of much-needed basic research, by virtue of its public good characteristics, will depend upon government initiatives and funding.",""],[1,"184","5","Targeted Incentives for High-Risk Research","Government agencies, key universities and research institutions, and some large corporations will need to take the lead, especially in high-risk, blue-skies research, through well-targeted incentives, by committing a sufficient (and sustained) volume of funding to high-caliber institutions, and by means of prizes and awards.",""],[1,"200","7","Basic Research Investment Increase","The government needs to increase investment in basic research, push through university reform, raise the skill",""],[1,"201","2","State-Supported Basic Research and Innovation Leadership","China will derive more of its growth impetus from home-grown innovations that rely not only on the generation of ideas through cutting-edge basic research-with risky blue-skies research supported by the state-but also on the harnessing of these ideas by dynamic Chinese multinational firms with global brand recognition",""],[1,"225","34","Basic Research Investment","Sustained investment in basic research;",""],[1,"282","2","Public Institution Climate Research Support","It should also continue to invest more in public insti- tutions to research, analyze, and disseminate this information.",""]],"13":[[1,"184","3","Pedagogical Innovation and Flexible Online Training","with greater use of multimedia and flexible online training customized to the varying needs of students.",""],[1,"184","3","Participatory Learning and Institutional Autonomy Reform","institutions being encouraged to experiment with supplementary modes of participatory learning and given the autonomy to do so.",""],[1,"186","1","University Autonomy and Research Excellence Reform","China will need to endow its premier institutions with a measure of autonomy from government not only to succeed in stimulating urban innovation but also to ensure that they are disciplined by competition and indicators of performance and remain efficient providers of services.",""],[1,"186","1","University-Employer Skills Linkages","These universities must interact with employers to impart technical and soft skills as well as the latest industry know-how.",""],[1,"186","1","Cross-Disciplinary Postgraduate and Postdoctoral Program Expansion","China's front-ranked schools must mobilize the funding and staff faculty positions needed to sustain cross-disciplinary postgraduate and postdoctoral programs, introduce innovative approaches to imparting knowledge and analytical skills, and establish specialized, well-staffed research institutes, some of international standing.",""],[1,"186","1","University Entrepreneurship Development Initiative","An important contribution universities can make to innovation is to groom the entrepreneurs of tomorrow who can transform ideas into commercial products and services.",""],[1,"200","7","Workforce Quality Improvement Initiative","An emphasis on further reforming SOEs, improving the quality of the workforce, encouraging applied research in firms, strengthening the research infrastructure, and building market institutions to sustain the tempo of competition and facilitate the entry of SMEs may be appropriate.",""],[1,"200","7","University Reform","The government needs to increase investment in basic research, push through university reform, raise the skill",""],[1,"204","5","University Teaching Improvement through IT and External Partnerships","By harnessing IT and tapping the exper- tise and resources of leading firms, universi- ties can improve teaching, motivate students",""],[1,"225","15","Education Philosophy and Teaching Methods Reform","Reform education philosophy; improve education methods; Recruit top-grade faculties; Encourage free thinking and independent research",""],[1,"225","15","Faculty Recruitment and Academic Freedom Initiative","Recruit top-grade faculties; Encourage free thinking and independent research",""],[1,"225","16","University Autonomy Reform","Grant more autonomy to universities; Reform the evaluation and appraisal system of university; Reform the grant award and evaluation system of major R&D projects",""],[1,"225","16","University Evaluation and Appraisal Reform","Reform the evaluation and appraisal system of university; Reform the grant award and evaluation system of major R&D projects",""],[1,"225","19","Recruitment of First-Grade Scientists and Engineers","Design effective internal incentive mechanisms; Recruit first grade scientists and engineers",""],[1,"229","5","World-Class University Development Programs","China is attempting to groom up to 100 universities (including the 75 under the Ministry of Education) into top-flight, world-class universities-through the 211 and the 985 programs (buttressed by the 863 and 973 programs).","Ministry of Education"],[1,"232","11","Overseas Faculty Recruitment Incentive Scheme","Recruitment of Chinese and foreign faculty members from overseas to introduce higher-quality talent and greater diversity is ongoing with the offer of generous incentives;",""]],"14":[[1,"225","7","Human Resource Supply Enhancement and Entrepreneurship Promotion","Enhance the supply of human resources; Encourage entrepreneurship",""],[1,"301","1","Flexible Demand-Driven Post-School Competencies System","Building the human capital base will also require a post-general-education system that provides a more flexible and demand-driven set of competencies.",""],[1,"301","1","Technical and Academic Stream Integration Reform","By 2030, the barriers between technical/vocational and academic educational streams would be reduced, with more core skills for technical students, more practical and applied higher education, and greater crossover between technical and academic streams.",""],[1,"301","3","Urban Worker Active Ageing and Lifelong Learning Measures","Finally, by 2030, China would have extended the productive life of its urban workers through higher retirement ages, flex- ible work arrangements, and lifelong learning opportunities.",""],[1,"309","7","Demand-Driven Youth Competencies System","Building the human capital base for the higher-value-added China will also require a system that goes beyond basic education to provide a more flexible and demand-driven set of com- petencies for young people.",""],[1,"310","1","Technical and Academic Stream Integration Reform","barriers between technical/vocational and academic educational streams would be reduced, by extending the period of development of core competencies for technical students, building a more practical and applied higher education curriculum in universities, and allowing for crossover between technical and academic streams within an overall qualifications framework.",""],[1,"310","3","Extended Working Life and Lifelong Learning Measures","retirement ages would be raised signif- icantly, flexible work arrangements would make continued work more attractive, and life-long learning opportunities for workers would help to reduce the depletion of their human capital as they age.",""],[1,"315","10","Technical and Vocational and Higher Education Reform","Deepening reforms of technical/vocational and higher education to promote a work- force with competencies that are relevant to the current labor market but also suf- ficiently flexible to adapt to the rapidly changing needs of the future labor market.",""]],"15":[[1,"79","3","Free Senior Secondary Education Expansion","providing free senior secondary level educa- tion will be essential.",""],[1,"301","1","Quality General Education Expansion","By 2030, China would have an education system that provides quality general education for all children from preprimary to the end of senior secondary schooling.",""],[1,"309","7","Expanded Universal Basic Education System","by 2030 China will have an education system that pro- vides an expanded cycle of quality basic education that is accessible to all, starting in preschool and continuing to the end of senior secondary schooling.",""],[1,"309","7","Fee-Free Senior Secondary Schooling","Senior sec- ondary school would be fee-free, as it is in most high-income countries;",""],[1,"309","7","Affordable Preschool Subsidy Scheme","preschool- ing would be available affordably to all, through subsidies for rural, migrant, and poor children as needed.",""],[1,"315","9","Phased Expansion of Free Senior Secondary Education","extending free access to senior secondary education in a phased manner that is consistent with fiscal possibilities.",""],[1,"320","6","Early Childhood Education Expansion","This section proposes measures to promote equality of opportunity in early child nutrition and to expand early child education, ensuring quality access for all.",""],[1,"322","5","Conditional Cash Transfers for Preschool Participation","A financing option that could be piloted on a wider basis is conditional cash transfers (CCTs) targeted to poor children to encour- age participation in preschool programs.",""],[1,"323","1","Pilot and Expand Locally Adapted Early Childhood Education Models","Piloting, evaluating, and gradually expanding different models of early childhood education suited to local fiscal and other conditions would be an agenda for the 12th Plan period.",""],[1,"323","4","Free Senior Secondary Education Expansion","is to move toward free senior secondary education, with sufficient public resources to ensure access and quality for rural, poor, and migrant young people.",""],[1,"323","4","Education Access and Quality Improvement Agenda","Many elements of the vision for China's education system in 2030 are already visible in the National Plan for Medium- and Long-Term Education and Development, including promoting equality of access at all levels of the system, enhancing educational quality, and promoting innovation and greater diversity in the education system.",""],[1,"379","17","Early Child Development and Education Interventions","Early child development and education covers children from conception to age six, and their parents, with a range of interventions including nutritional and health care interventions for young children and mothers to ensure optimal physical and cognitive development; early education programs-institution, community, and home-based-that aim to improve school readiness skills, including language, numeracy, and psychosocial skills; and parenting and caregiver education and counseling.",""],[1,"380","16","Additional Support for Senior Middle School Education in Impoverished Central and Western Areas","More assistance shall be granted to senior middle school education in impoverished areas in central and western regions.",""]],"16":[[1,"323","4","TVET and Higher Education Reform for Employer-Relevant Skills","The third direction would reform technical and vocational education and training (TVET) and higher education to promote a set of competencies that will continue to be in demand from employers as the economy evolves.",""],[1,"329","3","TVET Governance Reform","These include governance reforms to streamline and consolidate over- all sectoral policy setting and oversight.",""],[1,"335","12","Graduate and Post-Graduate Training Programs","Graduate and post- graduate training programs manage the attrition of workforce",""]],"18":[[1,"324","8","Free Tertiary Education for Teacher Deployment","The authorities have initiated free tertiary education to encourage teachers to move to western areas",""],[1,"324","8","Urban Teacher Rotation Programme","they are promoting rotation of urban teachers",""],[1,"325","2","Teacher Recruitment and Career Progression Reform","A key focus will continue to be teachers-teacher recruitment and career progression, allocation, compensation, and incentive policies.",""]],"21":[[1,"344","1","Promotion of Off-Farm Rural Employment","Higher rates of off-farm rural employment for those unlikely to migrate to urban areas should also be promoted.",""]],"22":[[1,"24","2","Hukou System Reform and Labor Mobility","China needs to accelerate phased reforms of the hukou system to ensure that by 2030, Chinese workers can move in response to market signals",""],[1,"24","2","Labor Force Participation and Wage Policy Measures","It also needs to introduce measures to increase labor force participation rates, rethink wage policy, and use social security instruments (pensions, health, and unemployment insurance) that are portable nationwide",""],[1,"61","2","Phased Hukou Reform for Labor Mobility","Phased reforms of the hukou system to ensure that, by 2030, Chinese workers can move to where the jobs are; measures to increase labor force participation rates; and reforms in labor taxation, hiring policy, and wage policy will all be essential to success.",""],[1,"61","2","Labor Force Participation Expansion Measures","Phased reforms of the hukou system to ensure that, by 2030, Chinese workers can move to where the jobs are; measures to increase labor force participation rates; and reforms in labor taxation, hiring policy, and wage policy will all be essential to success.",""],[1,"61","2","Labor Taxation, Hiring, and Wage Policy Reform","Phased reforms of the hukou system to ensure that, by 2030, Chinese workers can move to where the jobs are; measures to increase labor force participation rates; and reforms in labor taxation, hiring policy, and wage policy will all be essential to success.",""],[1,"61","4","Hukou Reform Package","a systematic approach to propel hukou reform forward would include: (1) delinking the hukou from access to public services and using a residential permit instead to determine eligibility to receive services; (2) encouraging pilot reform programs at the local level; and (3) redefining financing responsibilities between central and local governments as an incentive for reform.",""],[1,"61","6","Reduction of Social Insurance Contribution Rates","China should also reduce the level of social insurance contributions required for employed workers.",""],[1,"61","7","Equal Opportunity Public Sector Recruitment Reform","China's public sector entities should be \"equal opportunity employers,\" that is, their recruitment practices should be transparent, based on merit, and impose no restrictions on place of birth or possession of an appropriate hukou.",""],[1,"344","1","Labor Force Retention and Retirement Age Reform","This will involve adopting a mix of measures to ensure that older urban workers-particularly women- do not exit the labor force prematurely; these measures including raising the retire- ment age.",""],[1,"344","2","Labor Market Institution Reform","Building labor market institutions that allow for a sustainable balancing of the interests of workers with the need to maintain competitiveness.",""],[1,"344","2","Labor Tax Reduction and Wage Determination Reform","In particular, China should explore options to reduce labor taxation and to develop a more mature wage determination system.",""],[1,"350","5","Rainbow Project for Collective Contract Coverage","the 2010 regulations for the \"Rainbow Project\" of the Ministry of Human Resources and Social Security, which urges complete coverage of collective contracting in firms with trade unions by the end of 2012","Ministry of Human Resources and Social Security"],[1,"352","11","Wage Setting Reform Deepening","other reforms will need to be deepened to achieve a more uniform and market-based approach to wage setting in the formal sector.",""],[1,"353","2","SOE Wage Benchmarking Reform","For SOEs, the initial priority is to establish a robust system of benchmarking wages so that they are increasingly determined by reference to relevant comparators in the wider labor market rather than simply by the amount of profits available for distribution to workers.",""],[1,"353","4","National Strategy for Public Service Unit Wage Reform","it would be desirable in the short to medium term to develop a national strategy on reforming the process, including for social services.",""],[1,"354","2","Collective Bargaining Reform","To make progress in this direction, the role of the union as an effective voice of workers in wage negotiations will be expected to strengthen.",""],[1,"354","2","Labor Market Competition Reform","Gradual reforms in collective bargaining can be supported by efforts to make the labor market more competitive so that the relative bargaining strength of employers and work- ers reflects underlying supply and demand in",""],[1,"354","11","Performance-Linked Public Resource Planning Reform","PRP should not be used in isolation but as a tool to promote wider organizational reform, and the objectives and performance criteria should be set with this in mind.",""],[1,"355","14","Minimum Wage Setting Reform","China's challenge over the coming decade will be to shift from its current \"living wage\" approach with respect to the minimum wage to a \"wage floor\" approach, which is more common in OECD countries.",""],[1,"356","9","Labor Market Statistics Reform","Reforms of labor market statistics will need to be deep- ened, with regular surveys of representative samples of firms and workers.",""]],"23":[[1,"373","7","Aged Care Policy Framework Development","In the short term, the first need is to develop the broad policy framework for aged care services.",""],[1,"374","4","Aged Care Service Pilot Models","Piloting different models of aged care services to gain deeper understanding of cost and quality issues and to test different mixes of public, nonstate, community-based, and household provision and financing.",""],[1,"374","5","Aged Care Financing Model Reform","policy makers may experiment in the short term with a mixture of supply-side subsidies to providers and demand-side funding directly to households.",""],[1,"375","2","High-Level Aged Care Coordination Mechanism","elaboration of policy will benefit from a high-level coordination mechanism involving other players such as NDRC, MOH, MOHRSS, the National Commission on Aging, and others.","NDRC | MOH | MOHRSS | the National Commission on Aging"],[1,"375","2","Subnational Aged Care Coordination Mechanisms","coordination mechanisms will increasingly be needed at the subnational level to oversee, regulate, and administer the aged care services system and subsystems.",""],[1,"375","3","Aged Care Human Resource Capacity Development","Accelerated efforts to develop human resource capacity for the aged care sector are needed urgently.",""],[1,"375","3","Social Worker and Aged Care Professional Training Expansion","efforts are expanding to train social workers and other professionals, including gerontologists, aged care nurses, and other needed personnel.","MOCA"],[1,"375","6","Aged Care Regulatory Framework Development","Developing a regulatory framework for aged care services that encourages the entry of nonstate providers (community- based, commercial, and not-for-profit) but also ensures that basic standards of care are met.",""],[1,"375","7","Carers' Allowances for Dependent Elderly Care","Such pilots should be complemented by a system of carers' allowances for poor and possibly middle-income households who choose to care for dependent elderly members in the home or with the support of community-based organizations.",""],[1,"375","8","Integrated Aged Care and Assisted Living Models","Moving to integrated aged care and assisted living models that have a more differentiated range of services across the spectrum of needs and that draw on the skills of service providers from a wider range of agencies.",""]],"27":[[1,"16","5","Equality of Opportunity and Social Protection Expansion","Fourth, promoting equality of opportunity and social protec- tion for all.",""],[1,"24","6","Equal Access to Jobs, Finance, Social Services, and Portable Social Security","Fourth, expand opportunities and promote social security for all by facilitating equal access to jobs, finance, quality social services, and portable social security.",""],[1,"25","1","Expanded Public Services for Underserved Rural and Migrant Populations","Reversing this trend requires three coordinated actions: delivering more and better quality public services to under- served rural areas and migrant populations from early childhood to tertiary education institutions and from primary health care to care for the aged;",""],[1,"25","1","Social Security System Restructuring for Secure Safety Nets","Reversing this trend requires three coordinated actions: ... restructuring social secu- rity systems to ensure secure social safety nets;",""],[1,"25","1","Multi-Stakeholder Social Services Financing and Monitoring Mechanism","Reversing this trend requires three coordinated actions: ... mobilizing all segments of soci- ety-public and private, government and social organizations-to share responsibili- ties in financing, delivering, and monitoring the delivery of social services.",""],[1,"46","2","Expanded Financing for Basic Public Services","the government can invest more, drawing on resources it had previously devoted to infrastructure and manufacturing.",""],[1,"49","5","Equality of Opportunity and Social Protection Expansion","The fourth new strategic direction is to promote equality of opportunity and social protection for all.",""]],"28":[[1,"225","34","Social Security System Strengthening","Strengthen the social security system;",""],[1,"301","4","Universal Sustainable Pension and Health Insurance Coverage","By 2030, China would have full coverage of sustainable pension and health insurance systems, with more reliable security from all schemes and greater integration of all subsystems.",""],[1,"301","4","Aged and Long-Term Care Services System","In addition, China would have a well-developed system of aged and long-term care services that draws upon the human and financial resources of all stakeholders and provides a minimum level of service for the poor.",""],[1,"301","4","Coherent Safety Net for the Poorest and Most Vulnerable","Finally, the safety net for the poorest and most vulnerable would have greater coherence between different parts of the social protection system, providing acceptable coverage of benefits for poor households without at the same time creating \"poverty traps\" for the near poor.",""],[1,"310","3","Labor Tax Burden Reduction","over time, the burden of taxation on labor relative to other factors of production would be reduced.",""],[1,"310","3","Collective Bargaining-Based Wage Determination System","Key among these would be a wage determina- tion system driven less by administrative direction and more by collective bargain- ing, where the interests of workers, employ- ers, and the state are fully represented.",""],[1,"310","4","Integrated National Social Protection System","By 2030, China would have full coverage of pension and health insurance systems, with integration of currently separate subsystems for rural, urban, and migrant residents and for civil servants, PSU employees, workers, and residents.",""],[1,"311","1","Equalization of Basic Benefit Levels","leeway to equalize basic benefit levels across the country.",""],[1,"311","1","General Revenue Financing for Pensions and Health","general revenue financing would play an ongoing important role in both pensions and health, through subsidies to rural and urban informal populations, and tax-based incentives to formal sector workers to participate in supplementary pension schemes.",""],[1,"311","1","Aged and Long-Term Care Services System","China would by 2030 have a well-developed system of aged and long-term care services that draws upon the human and financial resources of the government, the nonstate sector, communities, and households, and that prioritizes a minimum level of service for the poor.",""],[1,"311","1","Integrated Safety Net for Poor Households","the safety net for the poorest and most vulnerable would have greater coherence between different parts of the social protection system so that it ensures adequate and affordable minimum coverage of benefits for poor households but without disadvantageg the near-poor and creating \"poverty traps.\"",""],[1,"315","4","Pension System Structural Reform","Helping them manage these risks requires structural reforms of the pension system so that people have adequate support in old age from a sustainable system, as well as deepening financial protection from health insurance; expanded pension coverage, in particular for rural",""],[1,"363","3","Inter-Provincial Pension Rights Clearing Mechanism","Moving to a system that can provide an effective clearing mechanism across cities and provinces is therefore an important but challenging agenda.",""],[1,"363","11","Portability Guidelines for Pension Entitlements","similar guidelines could be put in place during the 12th Plan period for portability of entitlements across all pension schemes to facilitate spatial and sectoral labor mobility.",""],[1,"364","1","Gradual Consolidation of Urban Social Security Schemes","portability of entitlements across civil servant, public service unit, and urban worker schemes would also not be straightforward but could be facilitated greatly by gradual consolidation of urban schemes, starting with PSU and urban worker schemes",""],[1,"364","2","National Pension Records Clearing Mechanism","To support the portability of both individual accounts and social pooling entitlements, a clearing mechanism would be needed for transfer of individual pension records and any necessary cross-jurisdiction or cross-scheme financial settlement.","MOHRSS"],[1,"364","4","Phased Consolidation of Provincial Contributions into National Pooling System","Realistically, it might be achieved in a phased manner with a gradually larger portion of provincial contributions pooled to the national levels over time as the information and administrative systems to manage information and funds flow are consolidated.",""],[1,"366","8","Nationwide Rural and Urban Resident Pension Schemes","In late 2009, China started rolling out a nationwide voluntary rural pension scheme that combines a matched contribution to an individual account with a basic flat pension benefit after retirement.",""],[1,"366","8","Stronger Inclusion of Migrants in Urban Workers' Pension Schemes","The Social Insurance Law of 2011 and labor legislation in 2008 also place stronger obligations on urban employers to include migrants in urban workers' schemes, and there are indications of progress.",""],[1,"368","1","Urban Pension Portability Regulation Implementation","While the recent portability regulation for the urban system is a step in the right direction, considerable deepening of the management information and payment systems for transfer of information and funds will be needed to operationalize the policy.",""],[1,"369","4","Integration of Public Sector, Civil Servant, and Urban Workers' Pension Frameworks","Taking preparatory design steps to inte- grate the PSU, civil servant, and urban workers' pension frameworks.",""],[1,"369","5","Integrated Pension and Compensation Reform","This would in turn require integrated policy development of PSU and civil service pension reform with broader compensation reforms.",""],[1,"369","6","Provincial-Level Pension Fund Pooling","Pooling contributions at the provincial level will allow for greater equalization of pensions within provinces and also strengthen the insurance function.",""],[1,"369","7","Pension Rights Portability Information Systems","Putting in place the information systems to facilitate portability of pension rights and pooling.","MOHRSS"],[1,"369","9","Integration of PSU, Civil Servant, and Urban Workers' Schemes","Completing integration of PSU, civil servant, and urban workers' schemes",""],[1,"370","1","Integration of Formal Worker Pension Schemes","To move to a unified scheme for all formal workers, it will be necessary over time to integrate the policy frameworks and managements of the schemes for the three sets of workers.",""],[1,"370","1","Supplementary Occupational Pension Top-Up Schemes","To avoid a sharp decline in income replacement rates for both PSU and civil servants' supplementary occupational pension schemes may be needed that could top up pension benefit levels in the integrated urban formal sector schemes.",""],[1,"370","2","Integration of Rural and Urban Resident Schemes","Integrating the rural and urban residents' schemes and their management.",""],[1,"370","6","National Pooling Reform for Urban Schemes","Raising the pooling level for the urban schemes to the national level.",""],[1,"370","7","Pension Financial Protection Reform","Taking steps to ensure sustainable financial protection of pensions.",""],[1,"371","1","Unified Pension System Reform for Rural and Urban Workers","This reform would require unified pension systems for these two types of workers across rural and urban areas, breaking down the historical locational divide.",""],[1,"371","4","Integrated Noncontributory Social Pension","The basic benefit pillar would provide minimum elderly poverty protection through an integrated, noncontributory \"social pension.\"",""],[1,"371","10","Defined-Contribution Pension Scheme Expansion","The contributory pillar would have a mandatory defined-contribution scheme for workers with wage incomes and a voluntary defined-contribution pension savings scheme for the urban and rural population with nonwage incomes (temporary workers, the self-employed, and farmers).",""],[1,"371","10","Notional Defined-Contribution Pension Option","For workers with wage incomes, a notional approach to the contributory pillar could be considered.",""],[1,"372","1","Voluntary Contributory Pension Scheme for Nonwage Earners","For those with nonwage incomes, continuing (and possibly increasing) government matching of contributions would provide strong incentives to participate in the voluntary contributory scheme.",""],[1,"372","1","Fully Funded Pension Scheme with Guaranteed Return","The scheme for those on nonwage incomes could be fully funded, preferably with a rate of return guaranteed by the central authorities to reduce the risk to contributors from empty accounts.",""],[1,"372","2","Voluntary Supplemental Pension Scheme","The supplementary pillar for urban and rural residents would allow voluntary occupational and personal pensions that could supplement other pension benefits.",""],[1,"372","2","Civil Servant and PSU Worker Pension Integration Strategy","It would also be a vital part of a strategy to integrate civil servants and PSU workers into the urban workers' scheme without a sharp fall in replacement rates.",""],[1,"377","5","Expansion of Basic Pension Benefits to Informal Retirees","with the expansion of basic pension benefits to rural and urban informal retirees in coming years, the interaction with measurement of household income for dibao needs to be examined closely",""],[1,"378","3","Social Security System Pooling and National Social Security Card","Increase real pooling level of social security system (pension at the provincial level, unemployment insurance and health insurance at prefecture level) and issue social security card nationwide",""],[1,"378","5","Universal Social Security Coverage Expansion","Establish full coverage of social security system to provide basic social security, upgrade pooling level of social security (pension and unemployment insurance at national level, health insurance at provincial level), and establish national standards for dibao and social welfare programs",""]],"29":[[1,"277","5","Ecosystem Health and Watershed Management Investment","Invest- ments in improved ecosystem health, biodi- versity, and watershed management will be sustainable with local people's engagement and employment through ecological protec- tion, restoration, and related payments for ecological services.",""],[1,"278","5","Payments for Ecological Services Expansion","For degraded ecosystems, expanded payments for ecological services in poor and ecologically important rural areas (for example, upriver watersheds or down- river flood plains) are needed (Bennett 2009).",""],[1,"281","2","Ecosystem Management and Conservation Investment Program","Therefore, complementary measures are required to invest in ecosystem management programs, protected areas, and watershed conservation.",""],[1,"291","8","Payment for Ecosystem Services Expansion","Expand payment for ecosystem services",""],[1,"291","26","Immediate Ecosystem Restoration Actions","Identify and implement immediate actions needed to restore ecosystem health",""],[1,"291","29","Natural Resource Management Sector Target Framework","Introduce targets for all natural resource management (NRM) sectors based on measures of ecosystem health",""],[1,"291","35","Key Biodiversity Areas Designation and Management Actions","Key biodiversity areas (KBAs) are designated and actions taken to begin management of these areas",""],[1,"291","44","Natural Forest Health Protection Measures","Urgent actions needed to protect forest health are identified and implemented in remaining natural forests",""]],"33":[[1,"16","5","Fiscal System Strengthening and Sustainability Reform","Fifth, strengthening the fiscal system and improving fiscal sustainability.",""],[1,"25","2","Fiscal System Strengthening Agenda","Fifth, strengthen the fiscal system by mobilizing additional revenues and ensuring local governments have adequate financing to meet heavy and rising expenditure responsibilities.",""],[1,"50","2","Sustainable Fiscal System Reform","The fifth new strategic direction is to build a sustainable fiscal system that will meet expected public finance challenges over the next two decades.",""],[1,"50","3","Fiscal System Strengthening and Reform","The efforts to strengthen and reform the fiscal system will also need to be aligned with the broader objective of reori- enting the role of government vis-à-vis the private sector and with the longer-term needs of a changing economy.",""],[1,"51","6","Municipal Fiscal Strengthening and Urban Tax Reform","Strengthen- ing the resource base of cities will require improved arrangements for tax sharing and transfer payments with the central government as well as new tax instruments, such as land and property taxes, that permit an elastic tax base.",""]],"35":[[1,"48","2","Market-Oriented Regulatory and Rule-of-Law Reform","as the government transitions away from direct intervention in enterprise and market activities and toward creating a policy and regulatory environment supportive of free and fair competition, it must also safeguard the rule of law.",""]],"36":[[1,"15","4","Strategic Framework for Reforms","World Bank would conduct research drawing on lessons from international experience as well as China's own successful development record, and prepare a strategic framework for reforms that could assist China's policy making as well as guide future China-World Bank relations.",""]],"40":[[1,"45","6","Broad Public Participation and Rights-Granting Reform","The government should respond proactively to these needs and grant rights to individuals, households, enterprises, communities, academia, and other nongovernmental organizations through clear rules that encourage broad participation.",""]],"46":[[1,"199","2","Venture Capital Industry Reform","This situation is being corrected, and the amount of capital contributed",""],[1,"201","12","Startup and SME Risk Capital and Mentoring Support","Increasing access to risk capital and men- toring of start-ups and SMEs by suppli- ers of venture financing",""],[1,"214","13","Green Development Financing Instruments and Vehicles","Fiscal instruments and financing vehicles (public and private, foreign funded) to raise the capital for the substantial up- front spending needed to jump-start green development; implement green urban proj- ects, many with long payback periods; and maintain the momentum of green develop- ment over the long haul",""],[1,"225","31","Financial Ecosystem Improvement","Create good financial eco-system; Keep balance market competition and regulation; Provide tax deduction for the capital invested in the high-tech enterprises",""],[1,"279","3","Private Capital Access Reform for Urban Infrastructure Investment","Remove barriers to private capital to stimulate private investment.",""],[1,"291","16","New Financing Mechanisms for Green Growth","Introduce new financing mechanisms to support green growth and steer cities away from land sales",""]],"49":[[1,"80","5","Provider Payment Reform","provider payment reform must be accelerated to increase the incentives for all health providers to produce efficiency and quality.",""],[1,"210","6","Health Care Innovation and Provider Competition Reform","China will have to strive for medical innovation that is tempered by effective regulation (which minimizes red tape, optimizes incentives for providers, and fully harnesses ICT) and for competition among providers on results so that more patients migrate to better providers.",""],[1,"301","1","Health Financial Protection and Provider Payment Reform","And the delivery system would be more accountable for the quality and cost-effectiveness of services through provider payment and other reforms.",""],[1,"301","1","Primary Care Network Restructuring","The restructured health system would be less hospital oriented and be built around a high-quality primary care network to meet more complex case management needs of noncommunicable diseases.",""],[1,"302","6","Rebuild the Health Delivery Network","China has moved actively to rebuild the health delivery network.",""],[1,"302","6","Expand Basic Health Insurance Coverage","It dramatically reduced the burden of infectious disease and expanded coverage of basic health insurance to almost all rural and growing numbers of urban citizens.",""],[1,"310","1","Health Financing Protection Reform","the health system would provide more uniform and deeper financial protection against health shocks for its population, with general revenue financing likely to play a bigger role in total health funding.",""],[1,"310","1","Provider Payment and Accountability Reform","the delivery system would have providers who are more accountable for the quality and cost-effectiveness of services through provider payment and other reforms.",""],[1,"310","1","Primary Care Network Strengthening","The reformed health system would be less hospital oriented than is the current case, and it will be built on a high-quality primary care network that is able to meet the more complex case management needs of noncommunicable diseases.",""],[1,"310","1","Integrated Patient Care Coordination","Case management would also involve greater coordination of patient care across levels of the health system.",""],[1,"315","2","Health Financing and Primary Care Reform","accelerat- ing reforms of health financing and pro- vider incentives and rebuilding China's primary care system as the basis of a sys- tem of coordinated care across levels of care to manage the epidemic of noncom- municable diseases and to avoid uncon- trolled cost escalation of health services",""],[1,"315","4","Health Insurance Financial Protection Expansion","Helping them manage these risks requires structural reforms of the pension system so that people have adequate support in old age from a sustainable system, as well as deepening financial protection from health insurance; expanded pension coverage, in particular for rural",""],[1,"316","1","Primary Care Incentive Reform","reori- enting delivery systems away from overre- liance on hospital care through incentives to promote use of primary care.",""],[1,"316","2","Health Care Financing and Purchasing Reform","Deepening reforms of financing and pur- chasing of health care services to promote more equitable health services for all citi- zens and ensure that the system provides quality services in a more cost-effective manner.",""],[1,"330","5","Health Financing and Incentive System Reform","reforming health financing and incentive systems;",""],[1,"330","5","Hospital System Reform","deepening reforms of the hospital system.",""],[1,"332","2","Pilot Incentive Harmonization and Referral Coordination","Pilots to build relations and harmonize incentives between hospitals and lower-level providers are under way (in Shenzhen and Wuhan, for example), but these remain in their infancy.",""],[1,"333","8","Hospital Reform Deepening","These include primary care reforms, deepening hospital reforms already under way, and addressing challenges in health financing and payment systems.",""],[1,"333","8","Health Financing and Payment System Reform","These include primary care reforms, deepening hospital reforms already under way, and addressing challenges in health financing and payment systems.",""],[1,"335","2","Health System Legislative Update","National legislation updated to reflect the modernized health system structure and features",""],[1,"336","4","Hospital Reform Deepening","Hospital reforms will need to be deep- ened.",""],[1,"336","6","Public Hospital Budget Constraint Reform","The key reform to be initiated in the short term is setting firm budget constraints on public hospitals.",""],[1,"336","6","Active Health Insurance Purchasing Reform","A key factor will be exerting greater influence over hospitals through more active roles for health insurance agencies as purchasers of health services that promote quality and cost-effectiveness.",""],[1,"336","6","Hospital Quality Contracting and Performance Monitoring","China has a growing body of local experience with contracts that set wider quality standards and form a good basis for further strengthening this function over time.",""],[1,"336","7","Hospital Governance and Management Evaluation","A second set of measures would evalu- ate the experience in improving hospi- tal governance and management.",""],[1,"337","6","Hospital Management Information Systems Expansion","The key in expanding management information systems, however, will be the need for",""],[1,"338","1","Hospital Management and Oversight Reform","efforts to involve greater third-party and community involvement in the management and oversight of hospitals could be accelerated in line with the growing numbers of pilots throughout China.",""],[1,"338","2","Public Hospital Competition and Nonstate Provider Entry Reform","A further reform, to be initiated in the medium term, will be the introduction of greater competition for public hospitals from the nonstate sector.",""],[1,"338","2","Public Hospital Management Contracting to Nonprofit Organizations","There is significant potential to contract out management of public hospitals to nonprofit organizations, as several high- and medium-income countries, including Brazil, have done.",""],[1,"338","2","Health Financing and Purchasing Reform for Nonstate Providers","To realize this goal, health financing and purchasing arrangements will benefit from proactive inclusion of nonstate providers.",""],[1,"338","2","Health Provider Licensing and Accreditation Regime","That in turn will require development of a licensing and accreditation regime to ensure minimum quality standards.",""],[1,"338","5","Health Financing and Payment Reform","Health financing reforms would benefit from a dual-track approach that focuses on reduc- ing disparities between schemes and between localities, while in the longer run further examining the role of social contributions and general revenues in health system financ- ing.",""],[1,"338","6","Provider Payment Reform","it is necessary to accelerate provider payment reform to increase the incentives for all health providers to improve efficiency and quality.",""],[1,"338","6","Health Provider Accountability and Professional Ethics Measures","Payment reform needs to be complemented with a stronger emphasis on professional ethics among providers as well as tools for greater accountability of providers to patients.",""],[1,"339","3","Provider Payment Reform Pilots","it is necessary to accelerate pilots on provider payment reform and evaluate their impacts on efficiency, quality, and health outcomes.",""],[1,"339","3","Transition Away from Fee-for-Service Payment","For all levels of care, the priority is to move away from the current fee-for-service model to alternatives.",""],[1,"339","3","Case-Based Payment Pilots for Hospitals","For higher-level care and hospitals, case-based payment is currently the main direction of pilots.",""],[1,"339","7","Nationwide Provider Payment Reform Rollout","another key medium-term reform will be to roll out nationwide provider payment reforms that fundamentally realign the incentives of providers and patients.",""],[1,"339","8","Doctor Salary and Compensation Reform","continuing reforms of doctor salary and compensation systems will be needed.",""],[1,"340","1","Delink Doctor Income from Prescription Volumes","Measures already being initiated to delink the incomes of doctors from prescription volumes are a welcome initial step in realigning doctor incentives with efficiency and appropriateness of care.",""],[1,"340","1","Reform Physician Salary Incentive Structures","a more fundamental reconsideration of the incentives embedded in physicians' current salary structures will be needed over time, even with case-based or other payment system reforms.",""]],"50":[[1,"301","1","Early Child Nutrition and Childhood Obesity Interventions","Underpinning this education system would be an early child nutrition program and expansion of childhood obesity interventions.",""],[1,"309","7","Early Child Nutrition Program","An early child nutrition program would ensure that chil- dren have a sound cognitive base to maxi- mize individual and societal returns to education; over time this program would also address the health risks of rising childhood obesity.",""],[1,"315","2","Early Childhood Nutrition and Education Investment","expanding public investments in early childhood nutrition and education, espe- cially for rural and poor children",""],[1,"315","8","Early Child Nutrition and Education Investment Expansion","Ensuring that all children get off to a good start in life by expanding public investments in early child nutrition and education, especially for rural and poor children.",""],[1,"320","6","Early Child Nutrition Equality Measures","This section proposes measures to promote equality of opportunity in early child nutrition and to expand early child education, ensuring quality access for all.",""],[1,"320","10","National Child and Mother Nutrition Program","consideration should be given to rapid initiation of a national child and mother nutrition program.",""],[1,"320","11","Universal Nutritional Supplementation Program","Universal implementation of nutritional supplementation for children under age three and pregnant or lactating women in all rural poor counties, and ideally in central and western provinces, and for migrant children in urban areas.",""],[1,"321","2","Parenting Education Program","Inclusion of a parenting education pro- gram.",""],[1,"321","2","Community-Based Child Welfare Service Package Pilot","In China, the Ministry of Civil Affairs' Social Welfare Department will be pilot- ing community-based delivery of compre- hensive child welfare service packages.","Ministry of Civil Affairs' Social Welfare Department"],[1,"321","4","National Childhood Obesity Prevention Program","As part of the overall nutrition program, development of a national program against childhood obesity.",""]],"51":[[1,"235","13","Genomic Patient Profiling for Preventive Medicine","A profiling of patients would initially be based on the patient's genome, although later it could be done through the transcrip- tome.",""]],"52":[[1,"251","1","Solar and Wind Energy and Related Industrial Development","include solar and wind energy, together with upstream and downstream industries such as relevant equipment manufacturing and electric vehicle industries.",""],[1,"272","4","Clean Energy Competitiveness Improvement","will dramatically improve the competitiveness of solar power, wind power, and other forms of clean energy.",""],[1,"283","6","Urban Energy Efficiency and Clean Energy Development","Cities should make a consistent and dedicated effort to reduce carbon emissions by sustaining demand-side energy efficiency measures-particularly in industry, power, heating, and buildings.",""],[1,"283","6","Urban Rooftop Solar and Solar Water Heating Deployment","In addition, clean sources of energy supply can be developed within cities, with roof- top solar PV and solar water heating installations.",""],[1,"290","16","Renewable Energy Scale-Up in Competitive Markets","Scale up renewable energy in a competitive market",""],[1,"290","19","Fossil Fuel Consumption Cap and Carbon Capture Deployment","Cap fossil fuel consumption and deploy carbon capture and sequestration on a large scale",""]],"53":[[1,"363","7","Nationwide Renewable Portfolio Standards Scale-Up","Scale up RPS nationwide",""]],"54":[[1,"16","5","Green Development Growth Strategy","Third, looking to green development as a significant new growth opportunity.",""],[1,"24","5","Green Development Incentives and Public Investment Package","Third, seize the opportunity to \"go green\" through a mix of market incentives, regula- tions, public investments, industrial policy, and institutional development.",""],[1,"24","5","Low-Pollution Industry Investment Promotion","The intention is to encourage new investments in a range of low-pollution, energy- and resource-efficient industries that would lead to greener develop- ment, spur investments in related upstream and downstream manufacturing and services, and build international competitive advantage in a global sunrise industry.",""],[1,"24","5","Green Technology Scaling and R&D Utilization","These policies have the potential to succeed, given China many advantages-its large market size that will allow rapid scaling up of successful tech- nologies to achieve economies of scale and reduced unit costs; a high investment rate that will permit rapid replacement of old, ineffi- cient, and environmentally damaging capital stock; its growing and dynamic private sector that will respond to new signals from govern- ment, provided it gets access to adequate lev- els of finance; and a relatively well-developed research and development infrastructure that can be harnessed to reach and then expand the \"green\" technology frontier.",""],[1,"46","2","Green Development and Environmental Action","Not only will green development improve the quality of life in China, it will contribute to global efforts at mitigating climate change.",""]],"58":[[1,"330","5","Health System Reform","China's Health System Reform initiated in 2009 recognizes many of these challenges.",""],[1,"330","5","Primary and Preventive Care Delivery Reorientation","reorienting the delivery system to put a greater emphasis on primary and preventive care;",""],[1,"333","8","Primary Care Reform","These include primary care reforms, deepening hospital reforms already under way, and addressing challenges in health financing and payment systems.",""],[1,"334","9","New Institutional Arrangements for Primary Care Providers","Experiment with new institutional arrangements for new primary care providers, learning from Chinese pilots and international experience.",""],[1,"334","9","Strengthening and Restructuring Rural Township Health Centers","In rural settings, township health centers with links to village clinics are the natural unit to be strengthened or restructured to provide integrated primary care.",""],[1,"334","9","Conversion of Township Health Centers into Ambulatory Care Centers","One possibility could be to convert THCs into ambulatory care centers, with family practitioners providing technical oversight of village doctors.",""],[1,"334","9","Urban Community Care Center Model","The urban solution would be built around urban community care centers.",""],[1,"334","9","Pilot Integrated Care Centers","Integrated care centers that include primary care providers along with other professionals (ambulatory specialists, social workers, geriatricians) should also be piloted.",""],[1,"335","22","Primary Care Resource Allocation Reform","Further reform the resource allocation model supporting primary care develop- ment.",""],[1,"336","3","Coordinated Care Reform Across Health System Tiers","Over time, China can move to a system of coordinated care across the three tiers of the health delivery system.",""]],"60":[[1,"250","8","Cross-Sector Climate Change Adaptation Planning","The risks posed by climate change will be addressed through proactive planning across all key sectors, including water, agriculture, urban, and health.",""],[1,"252","6","Green Development for Climate Risk Reduction","By anticipating climate impacts on agriculture, low-lying coastal areas, and areas vulnerable to extreme weather events, green development will reduce climate-related risks and improve investor and consumer confidence.",""],[1,"282","2","Weather Risk Information and Monitoring Enhancement","Already, China has invested consider- ably in its network of weather monitoring stations, and it has set up emergency alert systems in coastal areas.",""],[1,"282","2","Climate Data Improvement and Dissemination Investment","The country can continue to improve its understanding of past, present, and future impacts of climate change by increasing the quantity, quality, and accessibility of weather data.",""],[1,"282","3","Climate-Robust Technical Standards for Infrastructure and Physical Assets","Update and develop new climate-robust technical standards for valuable infrastructure and physical assets.",""],[1,"282","4","Strengthen Enforcement of Technical Standards and Building Codes","Strengthen the enforcement of technical standards and building codes.",""],[1,"282","5","Disaster Response System Enhancement","Enhance disaster response systems.",""],[1,"282","7","Disaster Risk Financing and Insurance Instruments","Offer insurance and other financial instruments to transfer risk and assist with recovery.",""],[1,"282","9","Mainstream Risk Management into Development Planning","Mainstream risk management into devel- opment planning.",""],[1,"282","9","Integrate Weather Risk Planning into Infrastructure and Land Use Plans","Planning for weather-related risks should be better integrated into infrastruc- ture and land use plans.",""],[1,"282","9","Risk Management Audits for Local Officials","Risk management audits should be incorporated into perfor- mance evaluations for local officials.",""],[1,"285","2","Public Investment in Weather Monitoring","financing public investments in domains where public goods, such as better weather monitoring, will not be provided by the private sector;",""],[1,"292","36","Weather Information and Warning Systems Investment","Invest in weather information and warning systems",""],[1,"292","40","Catastrophic Risk Financing Frameworks","Introduce financing frameworks for catastrophic risks",""]],"61":[[1,"9","13","Deposit Insurance Scheme Establishment","Establishment of a deposit insurance scheme",""],[1,"57","1","Orderly Resolution Framework for Financial Crises","in the event of financial crises emanating from inside or out- side China, adequate systems (including ade- quate capital buffers and clear accountabili- ties) will need to be in place for an orderly and systematic resolution of bad debts and either the recapitalization or the closing of insolvent financial institutions.",""],[1,"57","1","Decisive and Well-Coordinated Financial Sector Reform","financial sector reforms in the next two decades should be decisive, comprehen- sive, and well coordinated.",""],[1,"57","3","Sequenced Financial Sector Reform Road Map","financial sector reform should follow an implementation road map that is properly sequenced, and, because the financial sector will remain vulnerable to crises, be sustained as a priority through the next two decades.",""],[1,"57","7","Strengthening Financial Regulatory Bodies","the government needs to strengthen the independence, effectiveness, staffing, and funding of regulatory bodies, including supervisory agencies (at the central and provincial levels), and to give them greater enforcement and resolution powers.",""],[1,"57","7","High-Level Financial Stability Committee","A high-level financial committee on the basis of the existing framework could also be established, with the main objective of monitoring and managing systemic risk and maintaining financial stability.",""],[1,"57","7","Liquidity Support Limits and Standing Facilities","Limits need to be placed on emergency liquidity support to solvent banks facing short-term liquidity problems.",""],[1,"57","7","Enhanced Financial Stress Testing and Crisis Simulation","Stress testing should be continued and improved to take account of emerging risks, and \"financial war games\" should be conducted regularly to fine-tune the institutional arrangements for dealing with a financial crisis if and when one occurs.",""],[1,"57","7","Legal and Disclosure Framework for Troubled Banks","The government should estab- lish an efficient legal framework; insist on higher standards of disclosure, auditing, and accounting; and streamline the court sys- tem to deal with troubled banks and firms in a timely fashion.",""],[1,"57","7","Deposit Insurance Scheme","A functioning deposit insurance scheme to underwrite risks faced",""],[1,"58","3","Interest Rate Liberalization Monitoring Mechanism","Careful monitoring of the progress of liberalization will be crucial to ensure that banks do not indulge in destabilizing compe- tition that erodes margins or in reckless lend- ing that harms the quality of the loan portfo- lio, and that any emerging risk of distress is dealt with swiftly.",""],[1,"424","5","Central Bank Independence and Monetary Policy Transparency Reform","further moves to improve the independence of the central bank and to increase the transparency of monetary policy decision making would be essential to support greater international acceptance of the renminbi.",""],[1,"424","7","Financial Regulation and Supervision Strengthening","Strengthening of financial regulation and supervision is essential to ensure stability in",""],[1,"425","1","Financial Regulatory Coordination Improvement","Improved coordination among the various institutions involved in financial regulation will be important.",""],[1,"425","1","Market-Based Reform of State-Owned Banks","Further progress also will be necessary to ensure that state-owned banks operate on market principles, rather than being responsive to political decisions.",""],[1,"425","1","Nonbank Financial Institution Regulation and Supervision","At the same time, the regulation and supervision of nonbank financial institutions involved in these markets will be essential, particularly because, in the absence of such improvements, restrictions on the banking sector will be reflected in the transfer of funds to nonbanks.",""],[1,"425","1","Alternative Financial Instruments and Funding Sources","It will be necessary to encourage the growth of alternative instruments and sources of funds, while ensuring the soundness of all institutions that borrow short and lend long (and are thus potentially subject to sudden failure).",""],[1,"426","4","Central Bank Independence and Monetary Policy Transparency Reform","the government should proceed with steps to increase the independence of the central bank, improve the transparency of monetary policy, and strengthen financial sector regulation",""],[1,"426","5","Monetary Policy and Financial Market Regulation Reform","parallel efforts should be undertaken to improve the methods for con- ducting monetary policy as well as financial market regulation and supervision.",""],[1,"426","5","Financial Liberalization and Interest Rate Deregulation","the government can proceed with financial liberalization.",""],[1,"426","5","Non-bank Financial Institution Regulation Strengthening","Improving the regulation of non- bank financial institutions while establishing market-based interest rates is necessary to enable the government to manage macroeco- nomic policy successfully and ensure finan- cial stability.",""],[1,"429","4","Hard Budget Constraint and Local Government Borrowing Limits","hard budget constraints, that is, those that become insolvent would have to be allowed to go bankrupt",""],[1,"434","6","Integration of Basel Prudential Norms into Regulatory Practice","The government remains committed to integrating the Basel prudential norms into its regulatory practices",""],[1,"434","8","International Banking Supervision Information-Sharing","China also should take advantage of informal bilateral relationships related to international banking supervision to share experiences and information.",""],[1,"435","3","Active Role in Promoting Stable Financial Sector Regulation","China should, thus, play an active role in promoting more stable financial sector regulation.",""],[1,"441","11","Strengthened International Financial Governance Agenda","this agenda includes strengthened international governance; train- ing of supervisors for cross-border banks; the expansion of the Financial Stability Board; better regulation of credit rating agencies and private pools of capital (hedge funds); improvements in accounting standards; stan- dardization and increased resilience of credit derivative markets; principles for employee compensation; improved corporate gover- nance and prudential supervision (including macro supervision); reducing evasion of stan- dards through off-shore financial centers; and addressing the procyclical nature of capital requirements.",""]],"62":[[1,"9","19","Land Policy Measures for Rural-Urban Integration","Land policies to promote rural-urban integration in Korea.",""],[1,"24","2","Rural Land Market Overhaul","Finally, rural land markets need to be overhauled to protect farmer rights and increase efficiency of land use, and policies for acquisition of rural land for urban use must be thoroughly overhauled to prevent urban sprawl, reduce local government dependency on land-related revenues, and address a frequent cause of complaint from farmers",""],[1,"51","7","Urban Densification and Compact City Policy","The second complementary policy would be to restrain the geographic expansion of cities and increase the population density of cities.",""],[1,"52","3","Citizen Participation in Land Use Planning and Zoning","One way is to invite citizen participation in land use planning and zoning, which not only harnesses public creativity but also reminds officials to look beyond the interests of individual sectors.",""],[1,"58","4","Better Land Policy Reform","Better land policies will help secure farmers' rights over land use and promote agricultural investments and productivity; ensure that land is used for the highest productive purposes, whether in urban or rural areas; help reduce rural-urban inequality; and promote more efficient and sustainable urban development.",""],[1,"59","4","Agricultural Land Tenure Security Reform","The first priority is implementation of measures that ensure the security of agri- cultural land tenure, including rolling out the recent policy decision to grant indefinite land use rights to farmers, expanding land registration and strengthening rural land markets, and reforming and modernizing the governance structure of rural collectives and dispute resolution mechanisms.",""],[1,"59","4","Land Acquisition and Compensation Reform","A second pri- ority involves reforming land acquisition and compensation practices, including strategic land use planning at the municipal, regional, and national levels.",""],[1,"59","5","Legal Granting of Indefinite Farmland User Rights","In rural areas, the government could begin by legally granting farmers indefinite user rights to land they cultivate.",""],[1,"59","12","Land User Rights Legal Clarification Reform","the Communist Party's 2008 Policy Decision regarding this issue be enshrined in law soon.",""],[1,"59","13","Scale Up Land Registration Pilots","Over the coming years, various land registra- tion pilots need to be scaled up.",""],[1,"60","1","Land Registration and Dispute Resolution Reform","such markets will require improvements in land registration and documentation ... as well as the establishment of credible and trusted dispute resolution mechanisms.",""],[1,"159","8","Eligibility Rules for Indefinite Land Use Rights","The reform toward indefinite land use rights will need to clarify who will be eligible for such rights, for example through determining farmers' land- rights-related membership in a collective.",""],[1,"159","9","Land Contract and Certificate Documentation Reform","In the near to medium term, supplemental reforms will have to tackle the poor quality of current documentation on collective land ownership and individual use rights.",""],[1,"160","2","Land Registration Improvement","Improving land registration.",""],[1,"160","3","Scaled-Up Farmland Registration and Rural Land Titling","In the near term, the registration of farmland needs to be scaled up to realize its broader benefits.",""],[1,"160","3","Single-Agency Land Registry Administration Reform","Lessons from other countries indicate that administration of the land registry under a single agency could be one favorable option, because it avoids overlapping jurisdictions and parallel systems and approaches.",""],[1,"160","4","Development of Rural Land Markets","Developing rural land markets.",""],[1,"160","6","Farmland Rights Market Institution Development","The development of appropriate market institutions will need to proceed in parallel with complementary reforms, including household registration reform, promotion of labor mobility and expansion and access to social security systems.",""],[1,"160","6","Collective Land Tenure Security Strengthening","In due course, tenure security to other types of collective land and their marketability will have to be strengthened in similar ways.",""],[1,"160","6","Rural Land Use Rights Access to Urban Land Markets","In the longer term, rural land use rights holders need to be given access to the urban land market and become full participants in the sharing of benefits whenever rural land is being converted to nonagricultural purposes.",""],[1,"161","6","Rural Collective Institution Modernization Reform","Reform will need to clearly define the nature of collective ownership and unambiguously identify the",""],[1,"161","7","Collective Land Governance Reform","Such reform will not only require the strengthening of governance at the collective level but involve a fundamental redefinition of the remaining functions and roles of the collective entity as its responsibility for land management may no longer be relevant.",""],[1,"161","8","Rural Land Acquisition Policy Reform","China needs to introduce fair and transparent rural land acquisition policies and practices, cur- tailing the role of the state in land requisition, and ensure more socially compatible rural- urban integration by allowing collectives and land use rights holders to share the benefits from urbanization.",""],[1,"163","2","Urban-Rural Integration Programs","Local governments throughout the country are pursuing a range of programs aimed at promoting urban-rural integration.",""],[1,"163","8","Rural Land Requisition Policy Reform","the reform of China's current rural land requisition policies and practices is a pressing short- to medium-term issue",""],[1,"163","8","Rural-Urban Land Market Integration","integration of rural and urban land markets is required for more equitable benefit sharing in the longer term",""],[1,"164","1","Land Requisition Legal Framework Reform","In the near term, to be more in line with international practices, tighter constraints on the use of state's power for land requisition will need to be clarified in the legal framework and implemented",""],[1,"164","1","Urban-Rural Land Market Mechanism Development","Such reform needs to go hand in hand with allowing the emergence of market mechanisms that span the urban-rural divide.",""],[1,"164","4","Land Requisition Process Reform","reforms of the requisition process need to introduce key provisions such as advance notice, provide the opportunity for consultation and participation during the requisition process itself, and put in place workable channels for appeal and dispute resolution.",""],[1,"164","5","Land Market Integration and Benefit-Sharing Reform","Measures to decrease the urban-rural gap will have to consider fairer benefit-sharing approaches through, for example, gradually granting access for collectives or rural land-use-rights holders to participate in the transfer of rural land to the urban land market, while at the same time reducing and ultimately phasing out the state's monopolist power in the primary land market.",""],[1,"165","1","Rural Land Rights Protection","safeguard rural land ownership and, even more importantly, individual land use rights for farmland and residential land, in the process of urban-rural integration needs to be put forward.",""],[1,"165","2","Rural-Urban Integration Reform Pilots","Across China, several reform pilots are currently under way, most prominently in Chongqing and Chengdu.",""],[1,"165","2","Rural Land Rights Registration and Definition","These pilots are experimenting with rural-urban integration approaches and promote the registration and definition of rural land ownership and prop- erty rights",""],[1,"165","2","Rural Construction Land Transfer and Marketing Mechanism","the development of policies and mechanisms for the transfer and marketing of rural construction land for urban develop- ment",""],[1,"165","2","Labor Mobility and Social Protection Reforms","labor mobility and social protection reforms",""],[1,"165","2","Evaluation of Rural-Urban Integration Reform Pilots","the lessons and experiences of these pilots are to be collected and carefully evaluated over the coming years",""],[1,"165","3","Land Requisition Restriction and Unified Construction Land Market Reform","the state's power and scope for compulsory acquisition needs to be restricted to a more narrowly defined \"pub- lic interest.\"",""],[1,"165","7","Strengthen Urban Planning Checks and Balances","The weakness in checks and balances in the cur- rent urban planning system may be overcome by opening the planning process for public participation and scrutiny and transparency through mass media.",""],[1,"291","19","Flexible Land Conversion Quota and Reallocation Mechanism","Pilot a flexible land conversion quota and reallocation mechanism",""],[1,"291","20","Urban and Peri-Urban Land Market Development","Create functioning urban and peri-urban land markets in several municipalities",""],[1,"291","22","Land Use Regulation and Standards Reform","Change regulations and standards leading to inefficient land use",""],[1,"291","23","Urban Land Use and Mass Transit Coordination","Coordinate all new development and mass transit plans with urban land use zoning regulations",""],[1,"292","9","Land Consolidation through Improved Rental Markets and Supporting Institutions","Land consolidation through improved rental markets and supporting institutions",""]],"63":[[1,"24","2","Banking Commercialization and Financial Market Deepening","it would require commercializing the banking system, gradually allowing interest rates to be set by market forces, deepening the capital market, and developing the legal and supervisory infrastructure to ensure financial stability and build the credible foundations for the internationalization of China's financial sector",""],[1,"57","4","Interest Rate Liberalization and Exchange Rate Flexibility Reform","The first priority should be to make interest rates more flexible, and endeavor to move to a point where interest rates, not quantity controls, clear the credit market.",""],[1,"57","4","Exchange Rate Flexibility and Monetary-Macroprudential Policy Package","In addition, greater exchange rate flexibility is needed to help cool inflation and take pressure off reserve requirements, together with the deployment of a combination of monetary and macroprudential measures to pursue growth, inflation, and financial stability objectives.",""],[1,"57","5","Central Bank Open Market Operations Reform","This would mean an autonomous central bank adopting open market operations",""],[1,"57","6","State-Owned Financial Institution Governance Overhaul","Such reforms may require a thorough overhaul of governance structures in state-owned financial institutions, in which existing state ownership functions, agencies, and practices are reviewed, using lessons from examples of international best practice and failures.",""],[1,"57","6","Diversification of State-Owned Bank Ownership","In addition, efforts should be made to further diversify the ownership structure in state-owned banks and further reduce the shares held by the state.",""],[1,"58","2","Capital Market Deepening and Reform","the capital market will need to be deepened and reformed to make available more equity and securitized financing",""],[1,"58","3","Interest Rate Liberalization Plan","the Chinese authorities can also prepare and implement a plan that phases out the ceiling on deposit rates and the floor supporting lending rates; this step will facilitate the commercial- ization of banks and ensure stability in the financial sector.",""]],"64":[[1,"186","2","Urban Planning and Smart Green Urbanization","China needs to optimize the planning of urban development, build energy-efficient mass public transportation systems, provide affordable housing, and inculcate sustainable urban life styles.",""],[1,"213","8","Compact Urban Design and Public Transport Investment","it would put a premium on the compactness of cities and mixed-use neighborhoods with due attention to and investment in public transport systems, green spaces, and recreational amenities to reduce energy intensity as well as environmental pollution.",""],[1,"214","10","Urban Zoning and Compact Green Development Measures","Zoning, land use, floor area ratios, real estate and other property taxes (to limit urban sprawl), and urban design to arrest current trends; promote compact, green development; and begin transforming legacy urban infrastructure",""],[1,"250","5","Low-Carbon and Smart Urban Transport and Buildings Initiative","China's cities will have low-carbon and smart transportation systems and build- ings.",""],[1,"283","8","Urban Densification and Smart Growth Planning","Cities need to intervene in the shape and direction of their physical growth.",""],[1,"291","11","Coordinated Urban Development Policy Scale-Up","Scale up coordinated policies for land use planning, urban finance, and urban governance",""]],"65":[[1,"301","2","Hukou System Phase-Out and Entitlement Reform","Most critically, the hukou system would be phased out no later than 2030 as an instrument of allocating social entitlements to the migrant population.",""],[1,"301","3","Migrant Social Inclusion Policy Framework","the central authorities, need to play a more proactive role to encourage local efforts that promote social inclusion of migrant populations, financially and politically, through a common policy framework.",""],[1,"301","3","Portable National Social Security System","Labor mobility would be underpinned by a social security system where entitlements are fully portable across the country, and where pool- ing of risk at higher levels reduces the cross- regional disparities.",""],[1,"302","6","Hukou Reform","While hukou reform still remains an unfinished agenda, the strict constraints on population mobility have been gradually relaxed to release the largest flow of internal migration ever seen.",""],[1,"310","2","Flexible and Secure Labor and Entitlement Policy Reform","Flexible and secure labor and entitlement policies and institutions that promote an internationally competitive workforce where workers continue to share in growth and have labor protection.",""],[1,"310","3","Hukou Reform for Portable Social Entitlements","the hukou system would be phased out as an instrument for allocating social entitlements to the migrant population.",""],[1,"310","3","Portable National Social Security System","Labor mobility would be underpinned by a social security system where entitlements are fully portable across the country, and where risk pooling at higher levels has reduced the cross-regional disparities in social insurance protection.",""],[1,"343","13","Labor Mobility and Hukou Reform","Lowering barriers to labor mobility by ensuring the portability of pension and social security rights and phasing reforms of the hukou system so that all residents have equal access to a common set of social entitlements by 2030.",""],[1,"359","3","Parallel Residence Permit System","Establishment of a parallel residence permit system, which is intended to delink access to basic services for migrants from",""],[1,"360","2","Residence Permit Access to Public Services","In parallel, all migrants are encouraged to apply for residence permits to receive additional public services and welfare.",""],[1,"360","4","Localized Hukou Conversion Reform","Localized hukou conversion through exchange of rural and urban entitlements.",""],[1,"361","6","Phased Hukou Reform Strategy","Hukou reform needs a phased strategy implemented over an extended period, with the end goal being a simple population registration system, delinked from social entitlements.",""],[1,"361","6","Entitlement and Welfare Reform Preconditions","the necessary preconditions of entitlement and welfare reform that will reduce the importance of hukou, turning it ultimately into a more typical population registration system such as exists in many other countries.",""],[1,"361","7","National Residence Permit Reform Framework","The first step of the reform strategy could be to set a national framework for extension of the residence permit system, which would begin the process of delinking social entitle- ments of nonlocal residents from their hukou status.",""],[1,"361","8","Expansion of Residence Permit and Hukou Conversion Eligibility Standards","A second dimension of the reform is the spatial expansion of more liberal residence permit and hukou conversion eligibility standards.",""],[1,"362","19","National Residence Permit Rollout","the residence permit could be rolled out nationally sometime between 2015 and 2020.",""],[1,"362","19","Residence Permit Rights Equalization","the qualifying criteria and the pathway toward the same rights as the local hukou population could be accelerated over time.",""],[1,"362","19","Residence Permit Social Entitlements Expansion","a residence permit could by 2030 have the same social entitlements as those of local hukou populations and could be obtained after a reasonably short period of residence in a city with modest qualifying criteria.",""],[1,"362","21","Hukou Reform and Unified Population Registry System","Figure 4.33 summarizes a possible sequence of hukou reform that gradually expands the residence permit system to diminish the relevance of hukou, ultimately leading to a simple population registry system.",""],[1,"363","6","Pilot Residence Permit System","Pilot residence permit system",""],[1,"378","2","Local and Provincial Hukou Reform Evaluation","Deepen and rigorously evaluate the local/ provincial hukou reforms to inform national policy",""],[1,"378","2","National Residence Permit Framework and Standards","Develop a national framework and national standards for residence permit system and indicators",""],[1,"378","2","Local Hukou Reform Guidance and Promotion","Issue national guidelines and encourage prefecture cities to promote local hukou reform based on the national framework and guidelines",""],[1,"378","2","Residence Permit System Pilot and Scale-Up","Pilot the national framework of residency permit system and prepare for scaling up",""],[1,"378","4","Nationwide Residence Permit and Hukou Reform","Adopt the residence permit system nationwide and national guidelines for hukou conversion indicators, standards · Universalize open hukou access for rural residents in all prefectures, and liberalize/ standardize access standards for all intra- provincial migrants",""],[1,"378","6","Lowered Hukou Conversion Thresholds and Requirements","Further lower thresholds and requirements for hukou conversion based on cost-sharing and financing by central and local governments",""],[1,"378","8","Residence Permit Reform for Migrants","Apply residence permit to all residents to restrict the function of the hukou system to population registration",""],[1,"378","8","Open Access for Migrants","Open accesses to all migrants",""]],"66":[[1,"338","5","Basic Health Package Equalization and Private Insurance Expansion","it would be desirable to explore how increased funding at higher levels could help equalize a basic package of services that localities could top up with local resources and that could be further supplemented by expansion of private insurance arrangements.",""],[1,"338","7","Integration of Health Insurance Scheme Management","The first step in the short term is to inte- grate management of health insurance schemes within pooling areas.",""],[1,"339","1","Integration of Basic Medical Insurance Schemes","are already merging the management of the NCMS and urban residents' insurance schemes and have even achieved integration of the NCMS with insurance schemes for urban residents and urban workers.",""],[1,"339","4","Integration of Health Insurance Schemes","For the medium term, integration of the three health insurance schemes seems a sensible and achievable goal, initially within prefectures and provinces.",""],[1,"339","6","Provincial-Level Health Insurance Pooling Reform","the pooling level for health insurance should be raised to at least the provincial level and a common model of pooling defined to achieve more equalized health care coverage.",""]],"67":[[1,"378","3","Rural-Urban Integration Programs","Roll out rural-urban integration programs based on experiences learned from the national pilots in Chengdu and Chongqing",""]],"68":[[1,"393","2","Transition to an Open Financial System and Flexible Exchange Rate","the transition to an open financial system and a flexible exchange rate will require time to ensure that China's institutions are adequate to maintain stability in the face of shocks from the international financial system.",""],[1,"407","1","Transition to an Open Financial System","we would set a goal over the next two decades of transitioning to an open financial system, a flexible exchange rate, limited controls over FDI transactions, and a services sector that can compete with the world's best.",""],[1,"423","2","Gradual Liberalization of Capital Controls","Gradually liberalizing capital controls would enhance China's ability to achieve high-income status.",""],[1,"425","3","Renminbi Capital Account Liberalization Measures","The government is also undertaking cautious steps toward allowing foreigners to participate in capital account transactions denominated in renminbi.",""],[1,"425","3","Domestic Bond Issuance by Qualified Foreign Investors","A few qualified investors, including international development institutions in China, have been allowed to issue bonds in China's domestic",""],[1,"425","6","Financial Integration with the Global Financial System Program","A program to deepen China's financial inte- gration with the global financial system will require careful planning.",""],[1,"426","2","Gradual Financial and Capital Account Liberalization Sequencing","China can limit the instability often experienced dur- ing financial sector liberalization and capital account opening through a gradual approach that involves careful attention to the appropri- ate sequencing of policy changes.",""],[1,"426","3","Renminbi Exchange Rate Mechanism Reform","The most important first step is to reform the renminbi exchange rate mechanism in the direction of a more market-determined, flexible regime.",""],[1,"426","4","Market-Based Exchange Rate Reform","Establishing a market-based exchange rate",""],[1,"426","4","Exchange Rate Flexibility Policy","providing for exchange rate flexibility would improve the authorities' ability to control inflationary pressures through monetary policy and reduce the pace of the buildup of foreign reserves",""]],"80":[[1,"16","5","Open Innovation and Global R&D Linkages","Second, encourag- ing innovation and adopting an open inno- vation system with links to global research and development networks.",""],[1,"24","3","Open Innovation System and R&D Quality Upgrade","Second, accelerate the pace of innovation and create an open innovation system in which competitive pressures encourage Chinese firms to engage in product and process innovation not only through their own research and development but also by participating in global research and development networks.",""],[1,"24","4","University Skills and Research University Development","will need to focus on increasing the techni- cal and cognitive skills of university gradu- ates and building a few world-class research universities with strong links to industry",""],[1,"24","4","Innovative Cities Development Initiative","fostering \"innovative cities\" that bring together high-quality talent, knowledge networks, dynamic firms, and learning institutions, and allow them to interact without restriction",""],[1,"24","4","Patient Risk Capital Expansion for Startups","and increasing the availability of patient risk capital for private startup firms",""],[1,"47","3","Manufacturing Upgrading and Innovation Promotion","policies will need to encourage manufacturers to move up the value chain and advance rapidly to the global technology frontier (and, in some areas, push that technology frontier forward).",""],[1,"47","3","Global R&D Network and Marketing Integration","Achieving this will require further integration with the global economy and increased specialization, as well as participation in global R&D networks and marketing arrangements.",""],[1,"63","3","National Innovation System Institutional Reform","A better innovation policy in China will begin with a redefinition of government's role in the national innovation system, shifting away from targeted attempts at developing specific new technologies and moving toward institutional development and an enabling environment that supports economy-wide",""]],"81":[[1,"16","5","Global Market Integration Continuation","Sixth, ensuring that China, as an interna- tional stakeholder, continues its integration with global markets.",""],[1,"25","3","Deepen Trade, Investment, and Financial Integration with the Global Economy","By continuing to intensify its trade, invest- ment, and financial links with the global economy over the next two decades, China",""],[1,"25","4","Active Engagement in Multilateral Trade Negotiations","China must remain proactive in resuscitating the stalled Doha multilateral trade negotiations, advocate \"open regionalism\" as a feature of regional trading arrangements, and support a multilateral agreement on investment flows.",""],[1,"25","4","Gradual Capital Account Opening and Financial Sector Integration","Integrating the Chinese financial sector with the global financial system, which will involve opening the capital account (among other things), will need to be undertaken steadily and with considerable care, but it will be a key step toward internationalizing the renminbi as a global reserve currency.",""],[1,"25","4","Multilateral Engagement on Global Governance and Development Finance","China must play a central role in engaging with its partners in multilateral settings to shape the global governance agenda and address pressing global economic issues such as climate change, global financial stability, and a more effective international aid architecture that serves the cause of development in poor nations less fortunate than China.",""],[1,"46","2","Capacity Building for Global Engagement","it will need to align its national interests to global con- cerns and build its capacity to participate actively in global affairs and to design global rules instead of merely accepting them.",""],[1,"50","4","Global Market Integration and Openness Strategy","China must continue its integration with global markets even as it reorients the economy toward domestic sources of growth.",""],[1,"50","4","Proactive Engagement in Global Governance Institutions","China also needs to engage with global governance institutions proactively as an international stakeholder to help shape the global policy environment in a manner that is mutually beneficial for China and the world.",""]],"82":[[1,"55","2","State Enterprise Ownership Separation and Corporate Governance Reform","First, the government could securitize its implicit equity in state enterprises as soon as possible (in listed state enterprises, the value of the equity is already known). This would pave the way for state enterprise reform by separating ownership from management and introducing modern corporate governance practices, such as appointment of senior management, public disclosure of accounts in accordance with international practice, and external auditing.",""],[1,"55","2","State Asset Management Companies Establishment","Second, the government could consider establishing several state asset management companies (SAMCs) that would represent the government as shareholder and would professionally manage and trade these assets in financial markets where feasible.",""],[1,"55","2","Transfer of State Assets to the National Pension Fund","Finally, a portion of state assets could be transferred to the national pension fund with the flow of returns being used to help meet future pension obligations.",""],[1,"55","4","State-Owned Asset Governance Reform","it is critical, for example, that the State- Owned Assets Supervision and Administra- tion Commission (SASAC) confine itself to policy making and oversight, leaving asset management to the SAMCs.","State- Owned Assets Supervision and Administration Commission (SASAC)"],[1,"55","4","Professional Management and Performance Benchmarking for SAMCs","The SAMCs should have clear mandates, be indepen- dently and professionally managed, and be subject to publicly announced performance benchmarks (depending on the nature of the enterprises in their portfolio).",""],[1,"55","4","Transparency Standards for SAMCs","In addi- tion, they will need to adhere to international standards for transparency, including on operations and results, value creation, profit- ability, and dividend payments.",""]],"83":[[1,"46","2","Public-Private Partnerships for Public Service Delivery","Encouraging the private provision of public goods and services and forming public-private partnerships where appropriate will not only inject new skills and ideas into public service delivery but also empower the private sector and encourage greater participation in the development process.",""],[1,"52","3","Citizen Report Card Service Satisfaction Survey System","Another way is through designing citizen report cards and other similar methods of surveying that regularly assess public satisfaction with the level and quality of public services and seek",""],[1,"52","4","Support for Social and Voluntary Service Delivery","A third way is to support the participation of social and vol- untary organizations in delivering services (including health and education).",""],[1,"55","6","Private Provision of Public Services Policy","policies should encourage the private provision of public services",""],[1,"55","6","Public Procurement Reform","public procurement should be further",""]],"84":[[1,"166","5","Municipal Finance Reform and Property Tax Introduction","China needs to address the distortions in its municipal governance and finance system and reduce its excessive reliance on land-transfer-based revenue generation through the introduction of property taxes and reform of land requisition practices.",""],[1,"166","10","Market-Value-Based Property Tax Reform","Introducing market-value-based property taxes.",""],[1,"167","4","Property Tax Implementation and Simplification Reform","The development of such institutional capacity could begin in the near term.",""],[1,"284","3","Local Property Tax Reform","Adjusting the tax structure by establish- ing stable local revenue sources (such as a property tax, not land sales) is fundamental to more efficient land use.",""]],"85":[[1,"169","6","Industrial Structure Adjustment Catalogue","the \"Guiding Catalogue of Industrial Structure Adjustment\" and \"Catalogue of Industries for Guiding Foreign Investment\" periodically issued by the NDRC are comprehensive policies, identifying industries to be \"encouraged,\" \"restricted,\" or \"prohibited.\"","NDRC"],[1,"169","6","Industrial Policy Guidance for Specific Sectors","The NDRC also issues industrial policy guidance for specific sectors.","NDRC"],[1,"248","10","Strategic Industry Development Focus","The 12th 5YP has a strong focus on seven strategic industries-environmental protection and energy efficiency, new energy, next generation information technology,",""]],"86":[[1,"176","10","National Social Security Fund Budget Compilation","In 2010, the State Council began compiling a national budget for social security funds on a trial basis.","State Council"],[1,"176","10","Expansion of National Budget Coverage for Social Security Funds","The eventual aim would be to bring other social security funds within the control of the national budget.",""],[1,"176","10","Government Funds Revenue Inclusion in Fiscal Budget","From 2011, the Provisional Rule on Government Funds Management includes a stipulation that all government funds revenues should be included in the fiscal budget and subject to the scrutiny of the National Peoples' Congress.",""]],"87":[[1,"185","4","Patent System Reform and Intellectual Property Protection","In this context, an efficient and discriminating patenting system that learns from the experience of the U.S. and European systems (both of which are in the throes of reform) and effective protection of intellectual property, especially in technologically dynamic fields such as biotechnology, nanotechnology, software, and multimedia, will expedite the growth of China's innovation capabilities.",""],[1,"225","7","Competition Promotion and Intellectual Property Protection Measures","Promote effective competition; Protection of IPR;",""],[1,"225","12","Competitive Market Environment Promotion","Promote a fully completive environment; Credible IPR protection; Enhance the supply of human resources; Incentives for establishing R&D facilities and investing in R&D",""]],"88":[[1,"186","2","Green Technology Research and Commercialization Support","Smart and green urbanization will stimulate both research on and the commercialization of green technologies.",""],[1,"186","2","Demand-Side Innovation Instruments","Demand-side instruments such as government procurement and stan- dard setting can also spur innovation.",""],[1,"213","4","Innovation Promotion and New Product Development","The GPT should promote invention and provide the foundation for new products, processes, and related organizational and institutional changes.",""],[1,"214","12","Green Technology Promotion Measures","Standards and codes for structures and equipment; eco-labeling; incentives to use green energy; and education programs to stimulate use of green technologies",""],[1,"279","2","Promotion of Emerging Green Technologies and Services","Beyond the provision of market stimuli for transitioning to green development is the need to more actively promote emerging green technologies and services.",""],[1,"279","6","Targeted R&D Support for Strategic Industries","Offer targeted support for R&D, espe- cially in strategic industries, either through",""],[1,"290","18","Renewable Energy Technology Performance and Cost Improvement Investment","Invest in improving performance and reducing cost of existing renewable energy technologies",""]],"89":[[1,"186","2","Energy Pricing Reform and Environmental Standards Enforcement","Energy pricing reform and enforcement of national environmental and energy efficiency standards will create pressures to upgrade technologies, and urban development will be the main venue for introducing new construction materials and technologies for transport, heating, cooling, and many oth- ers urban needs.",""],[1,"202","2","Carbon Tax and Standards Enforcement Framework","Competition and market integration is inseparable from the efficient pricing of fos- sil fuels (with carbon taxes added to reflect externalities), electricity, and other nonre- newable resources as well as the setting of national standards (including environmental standards and standards encouraging energy efficiency) for products and the enforcement of these standards.",""],[1,"214","8","Local Environmental Tax and Fee Measures","Local taxes, fees, and other charges to manage energy and resource consump- tion as well as to raise revenues, including some earmarked for green infrastructure and development.",""],[1,"277","8","Resource Commodity Pricing Reform","First, reform pricing mechanisms for coal, electricity, gas, water, and other resource commodities to provide the basic market conditions for green development.",""],[1,"278","1","Resource Pricing and Subsidy Reform","most urgent reform so that prices reflect not only the market scarcity, but also as much as possible the external hazards imposed on the environment and health in the process of mining, producing, and using these resource commodities.",""],[1,"285","2","Green Growth Market Incentives","creating market incentives that alter current behavior and foster technological innovation;",""],[1,"285","3","Economywide Efficiency and Green Infrastructure Investment","Short-term gains will come from improved economywide efficiencies, to be achieved through efficient pricing (land, water, car- bon, and pollution), regulatory reform, and public investment in critical green infrastructure.",""],[1,"290","15","Energy Subsidy Reduction and Market Pricing Reform","Reduce energy sector subsidies and introduce market pricing",""]],"90":[[1,"191","10","Physical Infrastructure Investment for Technological Upgrading","An increase in R&D is being complemented by investments in the physical infrastructure supporting technological upgrading.",""],[1,"191","10","Multimodal Transport Efficiency Enhancement","Strengthening and more fully exploiting the potential of multimodal transport is helping to raise logistics efficiency.",""],[1,"191","10","Renewable Energy, Smart Grid, and Rail Investment Programme","massive investments in renewable sources of power, a smart grid, and rail transport are expected to reduce energy consumption.",""],[1,"200","7","Research Infrastructure Strengthening","An emphasis on further reforming SOEs, improving the quality of the workforce, encouraging applied research in firms, strengthening the research infrastructure, and building market institutions to sustain the tempo of competition and facilitate the entry of SMEs may be appropriate.",""],[1,"212","3","Research Infrastructure Investment","The science and technology capacity of China's coastal cites is well established and is being steadily augmented through rising investment in the research infrastructure;",""],[1,"212","3","Transport Infrastructure Investment for Connectivity","Moreover, leading inland cities such as Changsha are investing in the transport infrastruc- ture to improve connectivity, and all have",""],[1,"252","5","Coal Handling Infrastructure Improvement","Infrastructure constraints, particularly for handling coal, will be eased, and infrastructure investment requirements reduced.",""],[1,"279","5","Government Investment in Green Infrastructure","Increase governmental investment in criti- cal infrastructure for green development.",""]],"91":[[1,"204","1","Regional Innovation Policy Support for Inland Cities","its success will depend on regional innovation policies actively pursued by local governments, which must provide the incentives and build the institutions tailored to local needs.",""],[1,"204","1","Incentives for R&D Centers in Inland Cities","This process is already under way in Chengdu and Xian, for exam- ple,104 but its success will depend on regional innovation policies actively pursued by local governments, which must provide the incen- tives and build the institutions tailored to local needs.",""],[1,"204","1","Local Innovation Institution Building","local governments, which must provide the incen- tives and build the institutions tailored to local needs.",""],[1,"212","3","Regional Innovation Policy Development","that of several inland cities is now being developed through increasing attention to regional innovation policies.",""],[1,"212","3","University Upgrading and Local Cluster Development","Cities such as Xian, Chengdu, Zhengzhou, and Hefei are attempting to raise the profile of their leading universities, grooming local firms that could become industrial anchors for local clusters, much like ARM and Cambridge Consultants served as the anchors for the electronics clus- ter in Cambridge, U.K.135",""],[1,"212","3","Foreign Direct Investment Attraction for Manufacturing Facilities","Several cities such as Chengdu, Shenyang, and Chongqing136 have also been successful in persuading mul- tinational corporations to set up production facilities, which augment manufacturing capabilities and create the preconditions for a concentration of the value chain.",""],[1,"260","1","Regional Economic Growth Strategy for Huaihua","Huaihua in Hunan Province is endeavoring to take full advantage of improved transportation, telecom- munications, and logistics networks to pursue a new strategy for economic growth.",""]],"92":[[1,"207","3","Strengthening the Science, Technology, and Education Leading Group","Strong leadership by the Communist Party Central Committee (CPCC)/State Coun- cil, by strengthening the leading group on Science, Technology, and Education, headed by the premier, which would have the requisite authority.",""],[1,"207","7","Government Innovation Capacity-Building","Leadership and coordination must go hand in hand with a concerted effort to raise the technical and implementation skills of the government bureaucracies tasked with driving the innovation strategy.",""],[1,"225","35","Administrative Management System Reform","Reform of the administrative management system; Create a rule- of-law government; Responsive to the people's demand",""],[1,"283","4","Vertical Coordination Improvement","Second, improve coordination vertically, between the central state and provinces,",""],[1,"291","10","Pilot New Growth Pattern Reform Measures","Pilot new regulations, policies, and financing mechanisms to alter current growth patterns",""],[1,"312","2","Systematic Linkage of Local Pilots and National Policies","A more systematic linkage of bottom-up local pilots and national poli- cies can ensure that fragmentation is reduced and that national policies take local variation into account.",""]],"93":[[1,"208","1","Research Spending Evaluation System","The lesson from advanced countries is to start early by putting in place a system to rapidly evaluate research spending, to absorb the learning promptly into the policy-making process, and to be ready to make corrections or terminate programs that are not producing results.",""],[1,"208","1","Innovation System Data Analytics Capacity","China is at the stage where it can begin building the elements of an evaluation process into its emerging innovation system, learning from others and fully utilizing the latest available data gathering, storage, and analytic technologies that promise to make a difficult task more manageable.",""]],"94":[[1,"201","13","Innovation-Driven Government Procurement","Using effective and disciplined government procurement to stimulate innovation",""],[1,"208","4","Government Procurement and Standards for Innovation Demand","Demand-side policy instruments such as government procurement and standard setting for equipment and services, combined with adequate efforts to guard against protectionist and rent-seeking activities that undermine market competition and discourage high-tech FDI, will stimulate the demand for innovation.",""],[1,"225","34","First-Buyer Government Procurement Strategy","Create initial demands through the first-buyer strategy of government procurement",""],[1,"280","10","Green Government Procurement Standards","The most important and pervasive approach would be to introduce green standards for the roughly RMB 1 trillion ($155 billion) in government procurement each year, which can open up a huge market for green products and usher in a robust period of private sector growth.",""]],"95":[[1,"186","2","Open Competition and Responsive Policy Making","The key to success, however, will lie in genuine open competition supported by sound and responsive policy making.",""],[1,"214","1","Creative Green Growth Governance and Coordination","Fully exploiting the sources of green growth will be a function of creative urban and national bureaucracies committed to a green agenda and able to respond quickly to new information, formulate and implement policies, and achieve the necessary interdepartmental and inter-jurisdictional coordination",""],[1,"214","1","Civil Society Policy Oversight and Benchmarking","bureaucracies will need to be prodded by a vigilant, informed, and networked civil society to set targets, learn from best practice, and benchmark and produce results",""],[1,"225","39","Local Government Official Appraisal Reform","Reform appraisal system for local government officials;",""],[1,"279","5","Institutional and Governance Reform for Green Development Investment","Related to expanding government investment is address- ing institutional and governance issues (see goal 6, below).",""],[1,"279","9","Regulatory Monitoring and Enforcement Strengthening","The first need is to strengthen regulation generally by improving the monitoring and enforcement of existing regulations.",""],[1,"282","10","Strengthening Local-Level Governance and Institutions","measures are needed to strengthen local-level governance and institutions and to provide clear incentives in the direction of green growth.",""],[1,"282","10","Green Growth Incentive Framework for Local Government","measures are needed to strengthen local-level governance and institutions and to provide clear incentives in the direction of green growth.",""],[1,"282","10","Local Fiscal Revenue Reform and Performance Standards","New, sustainable sources of fiscal revenue and standards for evaluating the performance of local govern- ment agents are needed to provide the right",""],[1,"283","1","Green Growth Incentive Scheme for Local Governments","incentives for provinces, cities, counties, and townships to pursue green growth strategies.",""],[1,"284","2","Environmental Protection Incentives for Local Governments","Third, provide greater environmental protection incentives for local governments.",""],[1,"284","3","Green Growth Performance Indicators for Local Governments","The introduction of explicit performance indicators for local govern- ments that support green growth is also key.",""],[1,"284","3","Reform of Official Performance and Promotion System","Higher-level governments could reform the performance and promotion system to reward lower-level officials who place new and specific emphasis on measuring the qual- ity of growth and the sustainability in the use of natural assets.",""],[1,"284","3","Local Ecosystem Health Targets and Legal Authority Clarification","To make these indicators work, local targets for ecosystem health will be needed, and jurisdictional authority of various agencies responsible for meeting these targets need to be clearly defined by law in anticipation of possible bureaucratic gridlock.",""],[1,"285","5","Public Education for Consumer Behavior Change","scaled-up public education for all ages to help induce consumer and household behavior changes",""],[1,"291","14","Green Growth Performance Indicators for Local Officials","Roll out green growth performance indicators for local officials across the country",""]],"96":[[1,"247","2","Green Growth Stimulus and Energy Transition Plan","Its move toward green growth combines three mutually reinforcing objectives: responding to the latest economic crisis through a green stimulus, reducing its energy dependency, and rebalancing its economy toward green sectors in the long term.",""],[1,"247","2","Energy Intensity Reduction and Renewable Energy Expansion Targets","To rebalance this situation by 2030, Korea aims to decrease its energy intensity by 46 percent and to increase the share of renewable energy in total primary energy from 2.4 percent in 2007 to 11 percent.",""],[1,"247","2","Five-Year Green Growth Investment Allocation","Furthermore, the latest five-year plan allocates 2 percent of gross",""],[1,"250","4","Resource-Efficient Society Development","China will have established a resource-efficient society.",""],[1,"258","1","Development of Green Service Industries","First, it will give birth to new green service industries, such as ecosystem services, carbon asset manage- ment services, carbon trading, and contract energy management.",""],[1,"258","1","Economic Rebalancing Toward a Larger Service Sector","Second, it will sup- port the country's intended economic rebal- ancing away from heavy manufacturing and toward a larger service sector.",""],[1,"271","7","Green Industry and Technology Investment Strategy","For China to focus investments and innovation in green industries and technologies, it must pursue deep-reaching policy and institutional reforms and establish markets in which prices reflect scarcity as well as social and environmental costs.",""],[1,"275","5","Policy and Institutional Reforms for Green Development","these six barriers will have to be overcome through appropriate policy and institutional reforms that together provide clear incentives for changes in technologies, investments, and behaviors.",""]],"97":[[1,"68","3","Public Awareness Campaign for Green Consumption","increased public awareness will help shift consumer demand toward green products.",""],[1,"251","1","Innovation-Supported Green Market Incentives","new markets and incentives, sup- ported by innovation and research, will likely stimulate new low-carbon, resource-light, and environmentally friendly technologies, goods, and exports.",""],[1,"251","1","Public Awareness Campaign for Green Consumption","increased public awareness will help shift consumer demand toward green products.",""],[1,"251","2","Development of Green Support Services","specialized services are likely to develop that specifically support green development.",""],[1,"279","8","Green Consumption Promotion Measures","Encourage green consumer habits and increasing demand for green products.",""],[1,"280","8","Energy Efficiency Information Programs","A broader policy mix for energy efficiency could therefore include information programs (which can help to reduce anchoring effects and status quo bias) and quotas and technical standards (which can overcome the limitations of heuristic decision making).",""],[1,"280","8","Energy-Saving Social Norms Promotion","Similarly, promoting social norms in favor of saving energy and avoiding waste can help to change consumer behavior at relatively low cost.",""],[1,"280","9","Green Product Labeling and Standards Framework","And a fourth is to establish labels and standards for green products, services, and technologies so they are easily recognizable and understood by consumers.",""],[1,"285","5","Green Market Infrastructure and Information Support","infrastructure and information support to green domestic and export markets",""]],"98":[[1,"165","2","Farmland Protection Policies","policies for the protection of farmland",""],[1,"252","4","Agricultural Land and Water Degradation Reduction","Agricultural output will be enhanced by reducing the degradation of land and water.",""],[1,"291","39","Heavy-Metal Farmland Remediation","Remediation actions started for farmland that is most contaminated with heavy metals",""],[1,"292","11","Agricultural Subsidy Growth Limitation","Limit growth of agricultural subsidies (even if WTO-compliant)",""],[1,"292","15","Input-Saving Technologies Pilot Scale-Up","Scale up pilot schemes for input- saving technologies",""],[1,"292","19","Pollution and Waste Control Systems Investment","Invest in systems to control the most damaging forms of pollution and waste",""]],"99":[[1,"253","3","Energy-Efficient Building Exterior Installation","Install structrural exteriors that reduce heating and cooling needs for buildings",""],[1,"253","4","Farm Waste-to-Fuel for Cement Production","Use farm waste as fuel for cement making",""],[1,"253","7","District Heating Control Improvements in Buildings","Install better controls for district heating in buildings",""],[1,"253","8","Clinker Substitution in Cement Production","Substitute clinker for additives in cement making",""],[1,"253","9","Waste Heat Reuse for Coal Drying in Steelmaking","Reuse waste heat to remove moisture from coal used to produce coke in steel making",""],[1,"253","10","Variable Speed Pump Installation in Building Systems","Install variable speed pumps in HVAC and water supply systems for buildings",""],[1,"253","11","Waste Gas to Electricity for Steelmaking","Use waste gas to generate electricity for steel making",""],[1,"253","13","Building LED Installation Program","Install LEDs in buildings",""],[1,"280","8","Energy Efficiency Quotas and Technical Standards","A broader policy mix for energy efficiency could therefore include information programs (which can help to reduce anchoring effects and status quo bias) and quotas and technical standards (which can overcome the limitations of heuristic decision making).",""],[1,"289","7","Time-Limited Financial Support for Carbon-Intensive Sectors","Any support should be time limited, and communicated as such, to reduce fiscal costs and provide incentives for firms to invest in less-polluting technologies.",""]],"100":[[1,"214","14","Green Industrial Cluster Development Support","Technology parks, seed capital, and tax exemptions to induce the formation of green industrial clusters.",""],[1,"256","5","Green Technology and Clean Energy Vehicle Export Promotion","By 2030, the projected exports of green technologies and services specifically related to renewable energy and clean energy (mainly electric) vehicles is pro- jected to rise to RMB 1.691 trillion-2.916",""],[1,"258","1","Development of Green Service Industries","First, it will give birth to new green service industries, such as ecosystem services, carbon asset manage- ment services, carbon trading, and contract energy management.",""]],"101":[[1,"276","7","Transition Support Safety Net Policies","Safety net policies for those likely to be negatively affected may include income support, access to alternative employment, retraining opportunities, and relocation assistance.",""],[1,"281","5","Fiscal Transfers for Carbon Pricing Compensation","First, for regions most affected by policies to reduce emissions, compensation for carbon pricing (whether through taxation or tradable permits) could be made through fiscal transfers.",""],[1,"281","5","Progressive Social Tariffs for Essential Utilities","In addition, where price increases in water, electricity, oil, gas, and other markets specifically affect low-income groups, progressive \"social\" tariffs could be introduced.",""],[1,"281","7","Displaced Worker Retraining and Labor Market Mobility Policies","for displaced workers, job retrain- ing as well as labor market policies that per- mit workers to move to new jobs and loca- tions at relatively low cost will be needed.",""],[1,"281","7","Social Safeguards for Economic Transition","Managing this transition, and ensuring that the pace of change is well within the capacity of the economy to absorb, will require care- ful policy planning and proactive implemen- tation of social safeguards.",""]],"102":[[1,"277","7","Market Reforms for Green Development","The most pressing market reforms will kick-start the transformation of traditional sectors, start reducing environmental externalities, and mainstream long-term sustainability goals.",""],[1,"278","5","Market-Based Environmental Incentives Reform","Third, strengthen other environment- related markets and introduce market-based environmental incentives.",""],[1,"278","5","Strengthening Property Rights for Natural Resources","Property rights for water, land, and forests should be strength- ened, and market mechanisms for water, ecosystems, and land should be increas- ingly introduced.",""],[1,"279","2","State and Private Sector Green Industry Reform","Further steps to be taken involve both focusing the role of the state and opening up competition to the private sector.",""],[1,"285","2","Environmental Regulation and Enforcement","using regulation, backed by enforcement, to complement these market incentives;",""],[1,"285","2","Local Environmental Institution Reform","reforming and strengthening the local institutions that play a key role in allocating resources and managing environmental quality.",""],[1,"290","27","Resource Governance Reform","Deepen resource governance reforms; ensure clean water supplies and sanitation",""]],"103":[[1,"214","9","Carbon Market-Based Energy Consumption Management","Use of local or national carbon markets to manage energy consumption",""],[1,"278","3","Provincial Emissions Reduction Assessment Methods","Fourth, new assessment methods should be established for emission reductions by province.",""],[1,"278","3","Cross-Regional and National Carbon Trading Mechanism","Provinces should be able to reduce their actual carbon emissions, or achieve their reduction targets through cross-regional or national trading.",""],[1,"281","6","Equitable Carbon Trading Permit Allocation","if carbon trading is introduced, the initial allocation of permits, both by sector and across regions, can be done in an equitable manner with the specific cost of the low-carbon transition in mind.",""],[1,"290","22","Carbon Cap-and-Trade System","Implement carbon cap and trade",""]],"104":[[1,"279","10","Strengthened Standards for Green Technologies and Efficiency","Standards are a key regulatory area where improvements will shape behavior and create market incentives for green technologies.",""],[1,"280","9","Tougher Compliance Inspections and Market-Based Incentives for Standards","Compliance with standards can be increased through tougher inspections and buttressed by market-based incentives (such as insurance policies that require flood proofing or compliance with energy efficiency standards).",""],[1,"290","21","Energy Legislation Amendment and Regulatory Body Strengthening","Amend energy legislation and create a more effective regulatory body",""],[1,"292","37","Building Code Update and Enforcement","Update and enforce building codes",""]],"105":[[1,"284","1","Incentives for Smart Urban Planning and Risk Management","Incentives are needed to encourage smart urban planning and risk management by local and regional government agencies.",""],[1,"284","1","Strengthening Transjurisdictional Planning Bodies","Transjurisdictional bodies, such as river basin commissions and regional planning boards, should be given more authority.",""],[1,"284","1","Expanded Multi-Agency Membership for Regional Planning Bodies","Membership should be expanded to include representatives from multiple line agencies, local governments, and major users of natural resources.",""],[1,"290","26","Water Use Control Through Directives and Market-Based Instruments","Use directives and market-based approaches to control water use by main sectors",""],[1,"290","29","Consumption-Based Water Allocation Rights and Fees","Introduce consumption-based water allocation rights and fees for agriculture and industry",""],[1,"290","30","Expansion of Water Allocation Rights and Fee Programs","Expand water allocation right and fee programs to other major basins and industrial sectors",""],[1,"290","35","Expansion of River Basin Commission Membership","Expand membership in a major river basin commission to include a wider range of water users",""],[1,"291","6","River Basin Commission Expansion","Expand river basin commission membership for all major rivers; new financial mechanisms such as flood insurance",""]],"106":[[1,"290","32","National Rural Sanitation Program","Launch national rural sanitation program",""],[1,"290","33","Safe Drinking Water and Rural Sanitation Improvement","Ensure safe drinking water from the tap for large cities and improved sanitation for all rural people",""]],"107":[[1,"282","8","Agricultural R&D and Extension Services Investment","Invest in agricultural R&D and extension services to help make the agricultural sec- tor more resilient to the impacts of climate change.",""],[1,"292","12","Extension Services Expansion for Climate Risk Management","Expand extension services to better manage climate change risks",""],[1,"292","14","Extension Services Reform","Reform extension services",""],[1,"292","17","Producer Cooperative Reform","Reform producer cooperatives",""],[1,"292","18","Strengthen Producer Cooperatives in High-Value Agricultural Supply Chains","Strengthen producer cooperatives in supply chain for high-value agricultural products",""]],"108":[[1,"292","25","Waste Disposal Method Modernization","Phase out inefficient waste disposal methods, pilot new methods",""],[1,"292","26","Pilot Air Quality Management Plans in Major Cities","Pilot air quality management plans in major cities",""],[1,"292","27","Air Quality Management Plan Implementation","Complete implementation of air quality management plans",""],[1,"292","28","Efficient Waste Utilization Incentive Pilot","Pilot incentives for efficient waste utilization",""],[1,"292","31","Waste Utilization Scale-Up Initiative","Scale up waste utilization initiatives",""]],"109":[[1,"300","6","Social Services and Social Protection Accountability Mechanisms","Greater accountability would need to come through three channels: administrative systems that monitor performance and encourage quality and equity; market-based mechanisms that encourage private delivery and rely on competition with appropriate regulation; and direct accountability to citizens by increasing the role of civil society organizations.",""],[1,"302","2","Social Policy Reform Management","Implementing the necessary social policy reforms will need strong and sustained commitment to manage vested interests within the social sectors and beyond.",""],[1,"311","6","Stakeholder Participation and Co-responsibility in Social Policy Reform","These reforms should seek to promote deeper participation of stakeholders such as communities, nongovernmental organizations, and the nonstate commercial sector.",""],[1,"311","6","Expanded State Regulatory and Facilitative Role in Social Policy","To achieve this goal, the state will need new and more differentiated roles not only as a provider and funder but also as a regulator and facilitator.",""],[1,"314","1","Social Service Provider Licensing, Accreditation, and Regulation Reform","Achieving plurality of social service provision while ensuring quality will require the state to assume more complex roles in licensing, accreditation, and regulation of providers.",""],[1,"314","1","Regulatory Capacity Development for Social Services","Developing regulatory capacity in particular will be challenging, as historical challenges in achieving effective regulation in areas such as pharmaceuticals, food safety, and pension and housing fund management demonstrate.",""],[1,"314","1","Rules-Based Social Service Governance Framework","It will also require in some areas a willingness to \"step back\" from micro-intervention in the operations of social services and to focus instead on setting rules-based policy, regulatory frameworks, and sets of incentives.",""],[1,"314","2","Citizen Participation in Social Service Delivery","Increasing citizen participation in basic service delivery will require new ways of doing business for service providers and is also consistent with greater sharing of responsibilities in service provision.",""],[1,"314","5","Strengthened Cross-Sectoral Coordination for Social Service Delivery","Stronger intra- and intersectoral coordination in policy development and delivery of social services will be vital to improve social outcomes.",""],[1,"314","5","Administrative System for Cross-Sectoral Coordination","Developing an administrative system of cross-sectoral coordination requires the transformation of government functions and responsibilities.",""],[1,"315","2","Citizen Participation and Nonstate Service Delivery Expansion","enhancing citizen voice and participa- tion in the delivery and oversight of social services, and enhancing the role of the nonstate sector as service providers",""],[1,"316","3","Grassroots Citizen Oversight of Education and Health Services","Increasing the role of the population in oversight and some aspects of manage- ment of education and health services at the grassroots level to promote citizen voice and accountability.",""],[1,"316","4","Diversification of Social Service Provision Roles","An important cross-cutting theme in implementing the above policy and institu- tional reforms is diversification of the roles of state and nonstate players to promote greater plurality of social service provision.",""],[1,"316","4","Expand Nonstate and Community Sector Role in Social Services","If China is to realize its ambitious social service expan- sion and quality enhancement goals within its fiscal constraints, an expanded role for the nonstate (for-profit and not-for-profit) and community sectors will be essential.",""]],"110":[[1,"321","3","Child Poverty Reduction Pilot Funding Identification","Identification of funding sources for pilot and rollout phases.",""]],"111":[[1,"291","41","Wild Products Inspection and Enforcement Strengthening","Inspection capacity is doubled and penalties are increased for illegal import or sale of native or imported wild products",""]],"112":[[1,"324","10","Intergovernmental Education Financing Equalization","Further financing inputs from govern- ments above the county and district levels will be needed to avoid further widening of disparities in educational quality across provinces and within them.",""],[1,"325","3","Neighborhood School System Reform","The issue of how to integrate migrant children into a stronger neighbor- hood school system is one that will require policy reform as well as close focus on their educational needs.",""]],"113":[[1,"334","11","Health Provider Skills Development","Equip a new generation of providers with new skills, a process that will take a generation to complete.",""],[1,"335","21","In-Service Training Funding Mechanism Reform for Local CDCs","An important element of the in-service train- ing strategy would be reform of the fund- ing mechanism and incentives for local CDCs to provide essential public health services training at the grassroots level.",""]],"114":[[1,"340","5","Citizen Participation in Service Delivery Initiatives","initiatives to increase the role of citizens in promoting effective local services have expanded.",""],[1,"341","9","Citizen Participation Policy and Institutional Framework","a clear policy and institutional framework is needed so their participation is not ad hoc but is truly linked to formal decision-making structures and the incentives of providers.",""],[1,"343","3","Evaluation of Citizen Participation in Social Services","Rigorous evaluation of experience in citizen participation in social services at the provincial and subprovincial levels.",""],[1,"343","5","Institutionalization of Citizen Participation Processes","institutionalization of citizen participation processes and institutions, for example, through the adoption of provincial policies (such as in Shandong with parent councils) and assured budgetary",""],[1,"343","7","Citizen Participation Information and Education Campaigns","Information and education campaigns among citizens, officials, and providers on the benefits, challenges, and limitations of citizen participation along with practical guidance on how to initiate and institutionalize mechanisms.",""],[1,"343","8","Capacity Building for Citizen Groups and Organizations","Investment of public resources in capacity building of citizen groups and organizations to increase their effectiveness in social service oversight and management.",""],[1,"343","10","Conditional Citizen Participation in Social Service Financing","Use of provincial policies and guidelines on citizen participation in social service delivery and oversight as a condition of receiving central transfers in the education and health sectors, with a similar requirement for subprovincial levels as a condition for qualifying for provincial financing of social services.",""]],"115":[[1,"361","7","National Mobile Population Information System Standards","A second practical implementation challenge would be establishing national standards for information systems and data exchange of information on mobile popula- tions.",""],[1,"378","2","Population Registration Information System Platform","Start to establish a shared information system and platform from population registration and management",""],[1,"378","4","Population Information System Deployment","Deploy and roll out population information system",""]],"116":[[1,"349","5","Older Workers Flexible Employment Support","Facilitating access to part-time jobs and flexible work arrangements for older workers.",""],[1,"349","8","Employment Services for Older Workers","Strengthening the focus of employment services on placement of older workers.",""]],"117":[[1,"376","7","Convergence of Social Assistance and Rural Poverty Reduction Programs","The government's new rural poverty strategy points the way to greater convergence of social assistance, social services, and antipoverty programs over time (such as prioritizing dibao and near-poor households in training under antipoverty programs).",""],[1,"377","1","Convergence of Rural and Urban Dibao Programs","greater convergence of rural and urban dibao programs would be expected, with the gap between rural and urban thresholds within prefectures and provinces likely to narrow.",""],[1,"378","3","Equalization of Basic Public Services and Social Welfare Plan","Implement specific plan for the equalization of basic public services and social welfare",""],[1,"378","3","Rural Standards Upgrading and Policy Harmonization","Continue to increase the standards in rural areas to lower the gap to urban areas and harmonize key policies",""],[1,"378","5","Equalization Programs for Lagging Areas","Continue to implement the equalization programs and pay more attention to lagging areas",""],[1,"378","5","Rural-Urban Integration of Social Policies and Programs","Complete rural-urban integration of key social policies and programs (pension, health and health insurance, dibao programs)",""]],"118":[[1,"378","4","Migrant Service Provision Costing and Sensitivity Analysis","Undertake costing and sensitivity analysis of net costs of basic local-level service provision for migrants by type of city and volumes of migrants",""],[1,"378","6","Fiscal Sharing and Net-Settlement System for Hukou Reform","agree fiscal sharing arrangements for provision of basic public services to migrants and implement national net-settlement system to incentivize open hukou access by all cities through an incentive-based approach rather than an \"ordering\" approach",""],[1,"378","7","Hukou Reform Financing Integration","Incorporate hukou reform financing into budgetary management system and social economic plan",""],[1,"378","7","Public Service Budgeting Based on Permanent Residence Population","Make budgets for public services and social welfare based on the information of permanent residence population (>6 month)",""],[1,"378","7","Adjustment Fund for Receiving Areas","Arrange special budget and establish an adjustment fund to compensate the net cost in receiving areas",""],[1,"378","9","Standardized Budgeting Based on Permanent Resident Population","Establish a standardized budgetary approach based on the information of permanent residence population",""]],"119":[[1,"393","2","Trade Openness and Market Access Expansion","China should continue to pursue opportunities to increase its market access and maintain its relatively open trading system.",""],[1,"393","3","Support for Multilateral Trade Negotiations","China's huge size, its presence in most markets, the threat of rising protectionism, and limited regional agreements argue for continued support for a global trading system based on multilateral negotiations.",""],[1,"393","5","Multilateral and Regional Trade Liberalization Strategy","China should emphasize both multilateral and regional arrangements.",""],[1,"393","5","Regional Trade Agreement Participation and Trade Facilitation","At the same time, China needs to participate proactively in regional trade agreements that lower trade barriers at and behind borders and that intro- duce trade facilitation arrangements.",""],[1,"393","5","Open Regionalism Trade Policy","Where possible, China should advocate \"open regionalism,\" which would require that tar- iff levels agreed among regional partners be offered to other countries on a most-favored- nation (MFN) basis.",""],[1,"407","3","Open Trading System and Trade Liberalization Agenda","China should advance its own agenda in future negotiations, which could include reducing barriers to trade in services, seeking regional partners to achieve deeper integration, strengthening WTO disciplines to require \"open regionalism,\" and limiting the use of export restric- tions at times of rising food prices.",""],[1,"410","8","Pursue International Agreements to Open Markets","continuing to pursue international agreements to preserve and further open markets should be a cornerstone of China's trade policies going forward.",""],[1,"410","9","Advocacy for Larger Reductions in High Tariff Levels","the government should push for proportionally larger reductions in relatively higher tariff levels, rather than across-the-board tariff reductions.",""],[1,"411","6","Multilateral Market Opening Strategy","China should make efforts to push for the further opening of markets through multi- lateral organizations as a prime policy objec- tive.",""],[1,"411","6","Open Regionalism in Trade Agreements","China should pro- actively participate in regional trade arrange- ments and, where possible, strongly advocate \"open regionalism,\" which requires that tar- iff levels agreed among regional partners be offered to other nonmember countries on an MFN basis.",""]],"120":[[1,"393","5","WTO Government Procurement Accession Negotiation","China should also pro-actively push ahead with the negotiations for accession to the WTO government pro- curement agreement as part of its effort to improve procurement procedures, enhance transparency, reduce costs, and enhance quality in government purchases.",""],[1,"411","6","WTO Government Procurement Accession Negotiations","It should also proactively push ahead with the negotiations on its accession to the WTO government procurement agreement as part of its effort to improve procurement pro- cedures, enhance transparency, reduce costs, and enhance quality in government pur- chases.",""],[1,"439","5","Procurement Agreement Compliance Measures","countries must (for the transactions covered) eliminate discrimination against foreign suppliers, enhance ex ante transparency (advertising for bids for all procurements above a given threshold) and ex post transparency (explain why suppliers were chosen), and establish procedures to review complaints.",""]],"121":[[1,"425","2","Renminbi Trade and Financial Settlement Expansion","The government is taking steps to increase financial integration with the rest of the world.",""],[1,"425","2","Renminbi Use in Trade and Financial Transactions","Government policies are increasing the use of renminbi in trade and financial transactions.",""],[1,"425","4","Offshore Renminbi Market Development","The government is developing an offshore market in renminbi in Hong Kong SAR, China, including provision for renminbi balances to be held by foreigners, the development of instruments for hedging currency risk, and the issuance of renminbi-denominated government bonds.",""],[1,"425","4","Access for Foreign Institutions to Domestic Interbank Bond Markets","Since October 2010, China has allowed three types of foreign institutions, including foreign central banks or monetary authorities-renminbi clearing banks in Hong Kong SAR and Macao SAR, China, and foreign banks participating in cross-border renminbi trade settlements-to invest in domestic Chinese interbank bond markets using renminbi.",""],[1,"425","5","Expanded Offshore Renminbi Market and Trade Invoicing","An expanded offshore market and expanded trade invoicing will encourage some greater international use of the renminbi.",""],[1,"426","6","Renminbi Settlement Promotion Initiative","the government could increase current initiatives to encourage the use of the renminbi in settling current account",""]],"122":[[1,"422","5","Foreign Reserve Diversification Strategy","the authorities are making efforts to diversify reserve holdings away from dollar-denominated assets toward higher-yielding investments through various sovereign wealth funds, including the China Investment Corporation.",""],[1,"422","5","International Reserve Currency Diversification Discussions","China is also taking a lead in moving forward international discussions to encourage alternatives to the dollar as a reserve currency.",""],[1,"439","10","Foreign Exchange Reserve Diversification Effort","The government also is beginning efforts at diversifying foreign exchange reserves through the China Investment Corporation.",""]],"123":[[1,"437","5","Transparency and Standards Improvement in Aid Policy","The government should focus on improvements in transparency and standards over the medium to long term.",""],[1,"438","2","Aid Transparency Reporting Reform","One possible way to enforce an improvement in transparency would be to report the country's official finance according to DAC categories, as 20 non-DAC donors do already (World Bank 2011) or according to alternative categories that are consistent with independent aid flows.",""],[1,"438","2","Independent Project and Country Programme Evaluation System","Second, the government could introduce independent evaluations of projects or country programs.",""],[1,"438","2","Project Design Impact Research Integration","At the same time, integrating research on the impact of projects into project design would force officials to be explicit about the goals of projects and provide invaluable information on project results.",""],[1,"438","5","Aid Coordination with Donors and Recipient Countries","efforts to improve coordination with other donors will become more important.",""],[1,"438","6","Improved Management of Official Overseas Finance","China's official finance should take more factors into consideration.",""]],"124":[[1,"433","2","Market-Based Emissions Reduction Mechanism","China's earnest efforts to reach this target and establish a market-based emissions reduction mechanism",""]]}});